textosLlegan("theory", {"fil-t5":{"html":"<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>How to study this topic: ethics does not aim to give you ‘the’ correct answer, but to teach you to <strong>reason</strong> about how we should live. Pay attention to two questions that organise the whole topic: what makes an action good, its <strong>end</strong> (the consequences) or <strong>duty</strong>? The comparative table of the great ethical theories is the best guide for revision.</p> </blockquote>\n<p>In the street, 'moral' has another sense that it is worth not losing. We say that someone has 'high morale' when they face what comes their way with spirit, and that they are 'demoralised' when they have no strength left for anything. José Ortega y Gasset took this use seriously in 'Why I wrote <em>Man on the Defensive</em>'. For him, morality is not an adornment or a code added to life from outside. It is the state of someone who is in possession of themselves and performs according to what they can give. The demoralised person is not just someone who breaks rules: it is someone who has ceased to be entirely themselves. José Luis López Aranguren took up the idea in his <em>Ethics</em> (1958). Morality is a form of life that each person gives to their existence, and in it the disposition, the frame of mind with which we face reality also counts.</p><p>Let us think of two handball teams that are losing by fifteen goals. In the first, the players stop going to training, blame each other and go out to each match already giving it up as lost. The second reviews the video, changes the defence and returns to the court with a plan. The score was the same; what separates them is morale, in Ortega's sense.</p><p>This sense connects with Kant. In the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (A805/B833) he summarises the interests of reason in three questions: what can I know?, what ought I to do?, what may I hope? In the introduction to his <em>Logic</em> he adds a fourth, what is man?, and states that the first three refer to it. Ethics is not, then, separated from anthropology. Asking what I ought to do is also asking what kind of being I am and who I can become.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is morality a burden that limits us or an energy that makes us more ourselves? Can it be both at the same time?</p>\n<h2>1. Ethics and morality: the question of the good life</h2>\n<p>Every day we decide what is right and what is wrong. <strong>Morality</strong> is the set of norms and values that actually govern in a community (what is considered correct). <strong>Ethics</strong> is the <strong>philosophical reflection</strong> on that morality: it does not merely follow the norms, but asks <strong>why</strong> they are good and whether they are justified. That is why it is also called <strong>moral philosophy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Morality as a capacity and morality as content.</strong> The word “moral” comes from the Latin <em>mos, moris</em>, custom or way of behaving, and it is used in two senses that are best kept apart. In the first, morality is a <strong>capacity</strong> that we all have: that of evaluating what we do, telling right from wrong and judging our own conduct and that of others. In the second, morality is a <strong>content</strong>: the specific norms, values and customs that each society develops. In ancient Sparta, courage in war was the virtue par excellence; in our societies, freedom or equality occupy that place. The philosopher José Luis López Aranguren (<em>Ethics</em>, 1958), following Xavier Zubiri, put it as the difference between <strong>morality as structure</strong> and <strong>morality as content</strong>. Animals respond to stimuli according to their instinct; human beings, by contrast, have to choose between possibilities and justify what they choose, and that is why they are moral by their very make-up, even those who act badly. Being <em>immoral</em> (acting against norms one knows) is a way of being moral in the first sense; only a being that could not choose would be <em>amoral</em>, like a stone or, perhaps, an animal. The contents change from one age to another; the structure, the need to choose and to answer for it, is common to everyone.</p>\n<p><strong>Not every norm is a moral one.</strong> Over the course of a day we obey norms of different kinds. <strong>Moral norms</strong> bind us from within: whoever breaks them faces above all their own conscience, remorse or shame, and they apply even when nobody is watching. <strong>Legal norms</strong> are laid down by a recognised authority, they are written, and the State can enforce them: a fine, a sentence. <strong>Social norms or norms of etiquette</strong> (saying hello, not interrupting, dressing a certain way for a wedding) arise from the custom of the group, and their sanction is diffuse: mockery, rejection, no longer being invited. José Ortega y Gasset studied them in <em>Man and People</em> (1957) under the name of “usages”: nobody has decided them and almost all of us follow them without thinking. The three types overlap, but they do not coincide. Lying to a friend is immoral and hardly ever illegal; crossing an empty street on a red light at three in the morning is illegal and hardly immoral; turning up in a tracksuit to a job interview breaks a social usage, not a law or a moral duty. And sometimes they clash: for centuries it was legal to own slaves, and those who helped them escape broke the law for moral reasons. That is precisely what ethics asks: which norm weighs more when they disagree.</p>\n<p>Curiously, the two words mean almost the same thing in origin. ‘Ethics’ comes from the Greek <em>êthos</em>, character, way of being; ‘morality’, from the Latin <em>mos, moris</em>, custom, which Cicero used to translate the Greek word. The distinction between lived morality and ethics as reflection is therefore an agreement among philosophers so that they can understand one another, not something that lies in the words themselves: in everyday conversation they are often used as synonyms.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t5-4\">Its central question was already formulated by Socrates: ‘how should we live?’. It is not a technical question (how to achieve something), but a question about <strong>ends</strong>: what is truly worth wanting.</p>\n<p><strong>What does a moral norm rest on?</strong> The question “why shouldn’t I lie?” can be given very different answers. The <strong>religious</strong> one: because God forbids it. The <strong>traditional</strong> one: because our elders have always done so. The <strong>naturalist</strong> one: because it is natural, what suits our species or life in a group. And the <strong>rational</strong> one: because there are good reasons that anyone can examine; for example, that if we all lied, nobody could trust anybody. Philosophical ethics does not look down on the first three — many people live their morality out of a faith or a tradition — but it cannot stop there. The religious answer comes up against the question from the <em>Euthyphro</em>, which you will see in section 7, and it does not convince anyone who does not share that faith. Tradition explains where a custom comes from, not whether it is good: slavery was traditional too. And “natural” does not mean “good”, as Moore’s naturalistic fallacy warns. That is why ethics looks for reasons that can be put to anyone, whatever they believe; not because reason solves everything, but because it is the only thing those of us who think differently can share.</p>\n<p>To go further: in Book II of the <em>Republic</em>, Glaucon challenges Socrates with a story. A shepherd, Gyges, finds a ring that makes him invisible and uses it to do whatever he wants without anyone seeing him. If anyone would do the same, are we just only out of fear of being found out? The dilemma <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-giges\">‘The Ring of Gyges’</a> asks you to answer that question, which runs through the whole of ethics: why be just when injustice comes free?</p>\n<p>Behind Gyges lies a question of psychology, not only of ethics: do we always act out of <strong>self-interest</strong>? <strong>Psychological egoism</strong> answers yes: even someone who helps does so because it makes them feel good. It is said that Thomas Hobbes, on giving alms to a beggar, explained that he did it to relieve the discomfort that seeing the man suffer caused him. As early as 1726, Bishop Joseph Butler objected that this pleasure in helping <em>presupposes</em> that we already wanted the other person’s good: if we did not care, helping them would give us no satisfaction. And there is an underlying problem: if any action, even giving one’s life for someone else, is explained as self-interest, the thesis no longer says anything that can be tested. In the 1980s and 1990s the psychologist Daniel Batson designed experiments that support <strong>psychological altruism</strong>: someone who feels empathy helps even when they have an easy way out of doing so. Be careful: this is a thesis about how we <em>are</em>; <em>ethical</em> egoism and altruism, which you will see in the section on utilitarianism, say how we <em>ought</em> to act.</p>\n<p>It is worth distinguishing three levels of moral reflection. <strong>Normative ethics</strong> proposes criteria about what we should do (the great theories of this topic deal with it); <strong>applied ethics</strong> takes them to concrete and controversial problems; <strong>metaethics</strong> dictates no norm, but examines what moral judgements <em>are</em>: whether they can be true, whether they express facts or feelings. That is why we must separate <strong>descriptive relativism</strong>—the observable fact that societies disagree in morality—from <strong>metaethical relativism</strong>, the philosophical thesis that no moral judgement is more valid than another. The second does not automatically follow from the first.</p>\n<p>There are things we want in order to obtain others: money, to buy; the bus, to get to class. Others we want for their own sake. That is the difference between the useful and the valuable in itself. Values (friendship, truth, beauty, justice) belong to the second group. They are not tools for something else, but that which makes the world habitable and human. A world with many resources but without friendship or justice would be efficient, but it would hardly be a home.</p><p>It is worth distinguishing two senses of ‘interest’. <strong>Acting out of interest</strong> is using something or someone as a means to one’s own end. <strong>Taking an interest</strong> in something is devoting attention and effort to it because it matters to us. Aristotle saw this in connection with friendship, in Book VIII of the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>. There are friendships of utility, which last as long as the other is useful to us and dissolve when they cease to be so. And there are friendships of virtue, in which one loves the friend for what they are. Someone who only writes to you when they need your notes is not entirely a friend, even if they seem to be.</p><p>A close example: in a village they decide to clean and care for the river. They may do it to attract tourists, and it is not a bad thing that tourists come. But if they only care for it for that reason, the river will cease to matter the day tourists prefer another place. The person who cares for it because it is their river, because they bathed in it as a child and because a living river is valuable in itself, will continue to care for it even if nobody comes.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what would we lose if everything we value had to be justified by its usefulness? Can you think of something you value even though it is of no use to you?</p>\n<p>Metaethics asks four kinds of question. Some are <strong>metaphysical</strong>: are there moral facts, as there are physical facts? Others are <strong>epistemological</strong>: if there are, how do we know them? Others are <strong>semantic</strong>: what do ‘good’ or ‘unjust’ mean? And others are <strong>psychological</strong>: why does a moral judgement move us to act, when a judgement about the weather does not? The philosopher Bernard Williams (<em>Ethics and the Limits of Philosophy</em>, 1985) also warned against the temptation to reduce the whole of morality to a single principle. In an earlier article (1981) he had given an example: if a man can save either his wife or a stranger and, before saving her, asks himself whether impartiality allows him to, he has had ‘one thought too many’.</p>\n<h2>2. Freedom, a condition of morality</h2>\n<p>It only makes sense to speak of moral responsibility if we are <strong>free</strong>: if we were entirely determined, there would be no room for either praise or blame. Ethics therefore presupposes that human beings can <strong>choose</strong> and answer for their actions. Kant distinguishes between <strong>autonomy</strong> (giving oneself the moral law with one’s own reason) and <strong>heteronomy</strong> (receiving the norm from outside: fear, custom, authority).</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t5-129\"><strong>When is an act moral?</strong> Not everything we do can be judged morally: sneezing or tripping is neither good nor bad. For an act to be moral, three conditions are needed. It has to be <strong>conscious</strong>: whoever does it knows what they are doing. It has to be <strong>voluntary</strong>: nobody obliges or forces them. And it has to be <strong>deliberate</strong>: there has been a moment of reflection beforehand. If I help a classmate prepare for an exam, I know I am helping them, I have decided it myself and I have thought it was for the best. Behind it lies a three-step process: <strong>moral judgement</strong> (what is right and what is wrong), <strong>deliberation</strong> (weighing up the options and their consequences) and <strong>action</strong> (putting the decision into practice). Aristotle already studied the doubtful cases in the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> (Book III, 1). For him, what is done by <strong>force</strong> (when the wind or other people carry someone off where they do not want to go) or through <strong>ignorance</strong> (someone who gives a sick person a drink to cure them and kills them) is involuntary. But there are <strong>mixed</strong> acts: the captain who, in the middle of a storm, throws the cargo overboard to save the ship would not do so in normal conditions and yet it is he who decides it. That is why he answers for what he does, even though circumstances press on him.</p>\n<p><strong>A freedom with conditions.</strong> Nobody chooses from scratch. We choose with a body, a character, a family, a language and resources that we did not pick: these are our biological, psychological and social <strong>conditioning factors</strong>. But conditioning is not determining. Someone who grew up in a violent environment finds it harder not to repeat that violence, and does not thereby stop being able to choose; the fair thing is to take this into account when judging them, not to strip them of all responsibility. Courts do the same when they recognise <strong>mitigating circumstances</strong> (acting in a fit of rage, for example) or <strong>grounds for exemption</strong> (a disorder that prevents someone from understanding what they are doing). The word <strong>responsibility</strong> comes from “respond”: being responsible means having to account for what I do, before others and before myself. It looks back, to what I have already done, but also forward: whoever holds a post, cares for someone or has a new power within reach answers for what may happen. Beware of a misunderstanding: here we are talking about freedom <em>of the will</em>, the freedom to decide. <em>Political</em> freedom — that nobody prevents me from acting, or that I take part in common decisions — is another matter, and you will see it in Topic 6 with Isaiah Berlin’s distinction between negative liberty and positive liberty.</p>\n<p>If the world is governed by causes, can we still speak of freedom? According to the <strong>incompatibilism</strong>, no: we are only responsible if we had the <strong>possibility of acting otherwise</strong>. In a completely determined universe, responsibility disappears; hence <strong>libertarianism</strong> claims that, at the moment of decision, determinism cannot hold. <strong>Compatibilism</strong>, for its part, regards that requirement as unnecessary: we act freely when we follow our reasons and desires, without anything external forcing us, even if those desires themselves have causes. Certain thought experiments, such as those proposed by <strong>Frankfurt</strong>, seek to show that a person can be responsible even though no other effective option existed for that person.</p>\n<p><strong>Frankfurt’s example and the objection it received.</strong> In 1969, Harry Frankfurt published an article containing a thought experiment that became famous. Its most widespread form is this: Black, a neuroscientist, wants Jones to cast his vote for a particular candidate, and secretly has placed in his brain a mechanism that, if Jones were to lean towards another option, would force him to change course. However, Jones ends up voting for that same candidate for reasons that are entirely his own, so the mechanism never activates. He had no other possible way out and, despite this, we consider him responsible for how he voted, since it was he who made the decision. Frankfurt concludes that responsibility does not depend on having different options, but on the act arising from the person himself. From the libertarian standpoint, authors such as Robert Kane and David Widerker replied with a dilemma. If the mechanism is to act in time, it needs to pick up some prior indication of Jones’s decision. Either that indication already fixes what Jones will decide, in which case the example presupposes precisely the determinism that is at issue; or it does not fix it, in which case Jones retained the possibility of choosing otherwise at the final moment, so the alternative continues to exist to some extent. The controversy remains open for both camps. What neuroscience has added to it, particularly the experiments of Benjamin Libet, you will study in the unit devoted to metaphysics.</p>\n<p>The question of freedom has a very practical consequence: <strong>why and how should we punish?</strong> For <strong>retributivism</strong>, the guilty deserve their punishment because they freely chose to do wrong; Kant went so far as to write that, even if a society decided to dissolve itself, it ought first to carry out the sentence of the last murderer left in prison. For the theory of <strong>prevention</strong>, defended by Cesare Beccaria (<em>On Crimes and Punishments</em>, 1764) and Bentham, punishment is justified only if it prevents future crimes, and so it must be proportionate and never harsher than necessary. A third approach puts the emphasis on <strong>rehabilitation</strong>: the Spanish Constitution (Article 25.2) states that prison sentences shall be aimed at re-education and social reintegration. And if determinism were true, what would be left? Some, such as Derk Pereboom (2001), argue that punishment as something deserved would make no sense, but protecting society would, just as someone with a contagious disease is kept apart without being blamed; others reply that this would mean no longer treating people as responsible for their actions.</p>\n<h2>3. How are ethical theories classified?</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t5-10\">Faced with the question ‘what makes an action good?’, theories fall into two broad types:</p>\n<p><strong>Moral scepticism</strong> holds that there is no rational criterion for preferring some options over others or for distinguishing just from unjust. In morality, everything would be disguised tastes, customs, or interests. It is a thesis that must be taken seriously, because the extent of our disagreements seems to support it.</p><p>But let us look at how we speak. Someone who says ‘I don’t like broccoli’ reports a personal preference, and no one is obliged to agree or disagree. Someone who says ‘it is unjust to throw away food while others go hungry’ is not stating their taste. They claim that any rational and impartial person would recognise it, provided they take into account the interest of all those affected and not only that of their own group. That is why this statement is discussed with reasons: it is defended, qualified, or refuted. This is the difference between a <strong>subjective preference</strong> and a <strong>claim to universal validity</strong>.</p><p>Claiming validity does not prove having it: we can be mistaken. But the sceptic has to explain why we reason in this way and why moral reflection works in practice. Thanks to it we can guide a more just social life: criticise laws, distribute better, and protect those who cannot defend themselves. And also a more fulfilled personal life, by thinking about what is worthwhile. If all evaluation were mere taste, denouncing the unjust distribution of wealth in a world with hunger would weigh the same as complaining about a dish.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is ‘I don’t like broccoli’ the same as ‘slavery is unjust’? Why? What would you do to convince someone of the second statement, and what to convince them of the first?</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Material ethics</strong>: they say what the <strong>good</strong> or <strong>end</strong> to be pursued is (happiness, pleasure…). They are usually <strong>teleological</strong> (from the Greek <em>telos</em>, end): they judge the action by the end towards which it is directed. Not all are consequentialist: for Aristotle the virtuous life itself counts; utilitarianism, by contrast, judges only by <strong>consequences</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Formal ethics</strong>: they do not fix a content, but the <strong>form</strong> that every moral norm must have. They are <strong>deontological</strong> (from the Greek <em>déon</em>, duty): they judge the action by <strong>duty</strong>, not by its results.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Material / teleological</th><th>Formal / deontological</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What do they judge?</td><td>The end towards which the action tends (in utilitarianism, its consequences)</td><td>Intention and duty</td></tr><tr><td>What is good?</td><td>What leads to the end (happiness, pleasure, utility)</td><td>What is done out of respect for the moral law</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Aristotle, Epicurus, utilitarianism</td><td>Kant</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Two more ways of sorting the theories.</strong> The first concerns what is being judged. <strong>Ethics of the act</strong> asks what I should do in this situation and looks for a criterion for each decision: Kant’s duty or the utilitarian calculation of consequences. <strong>Ethics of character</strong> asks what kind of person I want to become: for Aristotle or the Stoics, what matters is not getting an isolated dilemma right, but forming through habit a character that gets things right almost without thinking. An example: faced with lying, the ethics of the act asks whether lying is permitted here; the ethics of character, what makes someone a sincere person. The second distinction is Kant’s (<em>Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals</em>, 1785) and separates two kinds of “you must”. A <strong>hypothetical imperative</strong> commands on a condition: “if you want to be healthy, you must do sport”. If you do not care about being fit, the command stops binding you: it is a piece of technical or prudential advice. A <strong>categorical imperative</strong> commands without condition: “you must not humiliate anyone”, whether you want to or not and whether it suits you or not. For Kant, only this second kind is properly moral, and that is why his ethics is formal: it does not depend on the ends each person pursues.</p>\n<h2>4. The great material ethics</h2>\n<h3>Aristotle’s eudaimonism</h3>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t5-15\">For <strong>Aristotle</strong>, everything tends towards an end, and the ultimate end of the human being is <strong>happiness</strong> (<em>eudaimonia</em>): not a moment of pleasure, but a life fulfilled as a whole. It is attained by developing <strong>virtue</strong>, understood as a <strong>mean</strong> between two extremes (courage lies between cowardice and recklessness), guided by reason and habit.</p>\n<p>A nuance that is often lost: Aristotle’s <strong>mean</strong> is not the arithmetical average. Between two and ten, the midpoint is six for anyone; but the right amount of food is not the same for an athlete as for someone who is starting to train, and that is why Aristotle speaks of a mean ‘relative to us’, fixed by the practical reason of the prudent person. Moreover, virtue is a mean if we look at how it is made up, but an extreme if we look at its worth: there is nothing better than it. And there are actions that admit of no mean, such as theft or murder: there is no moderate way of committing them. All this can be read in the original in <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-aristoteles-termino-medio\">‘Aristotle: virtue as the mean’</a>.</p>\n<p>Aristotle justifies his idea of happiness with the <strong>function argument</strong> (<em>ergon</em>): a good knife is one that cuts well, and a good human being is one who does well what is proper to human beings, which is to live according to reason. Many object that it is not clear that human beings have a function, as knives do. There is also a difficulty that Aristotle himself saw: we become just by doing just things, but how can someone who is not yet just do just things? His answer is that the learner imitates at first without understanding, like someone learning to play an instrument, until the habit becomes their own. Centuries later, Thomas Aquinas added the <strong>theological</strong> virtues (faith, hope and charity) to Aristotle’s, and today the <strong>positive psychology</strong> of Martin Seligman studies character strengths with a very Aristotelian vocabulary.</p>\n<h3>Epicurus’s hedonism</h3>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t5-17\"><strong>Epicurus</strong> identifies the good with <strong>pleasure</strong>, but not unbridled pleasure: rather the <strong>absence of pain and disturbance</strong> (<em>ataraxia</em>): a serene life, with friends and without needless fears (of neither the gods nor death).</p>\n<p>To go further: in the <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-epicuro-meneceo\">‘Letter to Menoeceus’</a>, Epicurus sums up his ethics for a friend. He begins by saying that it is never too early or too late to philosophise. He classifies desires as natural and necessary (eating, keeping warm), natural but not necessary (a banquet) and vain (fame, unlimited power). And he removes the fear of death with a brief argument: while we exist, death is not here, and when it comes, it is we who are no longer here. He ends by separating what happens by necessity, what is the result of chance and what is up to us, which is the only thing we answer for.</p>\n<p>Is Epicurus right to say that dying does not affect us? Thomas Nagel (1970) answered no, with the <strong>deprivation argument</strong>: death is bad for the one who dies because it takes away all the good things they would have lived. The fact that they do not feel it does not settle the matter; someone who is deceived without knowing it does not suffer either, and yet they have been harmed. Lucretius, Epicurus’s Roman disciple, had left a reply ready, the <strong>symmetry</strong> argument: nobody is distressed by the centuries in which they had not yet been born, and the time after death is just like that time. Nagel replies that it is not the same: we could have died later, but we could not have been born much earlier and still be ourselves. The debate is still alive: why do we care so much about the future and so little about the past?</p>\n<h3>Stoicism: what depends on us (Epictetus and Marcus Aurelius)</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Stoics</strong> start from a simple and powerful distinction: there are things that <strong>depend on us</strong> (our judgements, our desires, what we decide to do) and things that <strong>do not</strong> (the body, reputation, what each person owns, what others do). The mistake that makes us unhappy is insisting on governing the latter. Serenity comes when we put our energy only into what is in our hands and accept the rest. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/epicteto\">Epictetus</a></strong> (c. 50-135), who was a <strong>slave</strong> before he was a teacher, made this idea into a manual for living; <strong><a href=\"#biografias/marco_aurelio\">Marcus Aurelius</a></strong> (121-180), who was <strong>emperor</strong> of Rome, wrote the same exercises to himself. The slave and the emperor agree: freedom is not in the circumstances, but in the attitude.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Of things, some depend on us and others do not. Within our power are judgement, impulse, desire, aversion; not within our power are the body, property, reputation, office. — Epictetus, <em>Enchiridion</em>, 1</p> </blockquote>\n<p>Science has internal limits: what its method can find out and what it cannot. But it also has external limits, because someone finances it, someone decides what is researched, and someone uses its results. Therefore, it is not enough to ask whether a piece of research is correct; we must also ask what it is for. Two values help to answer this. The first is <strong>responsibility</strong>: science can be put at the service of economic or political interests that harm people, and the researcher cannot disengage from this. The second is <strong>personal and social fulfilment</strong>: knowledge is valuable when it helps us live better, understand the world, and expand what we can do together.</p><p>Ernesto Sabato earned a doctorate in Physics and worked at the Curie laboratory in Paris before leaving science for literature. He denounced this in essays such as <em>One and the Universe</em> (1945) and <em>Men and Gears</em> (1951): science is not morally neutral. A civilisation that only values what is measurable and efficient ends up treating people as parts of a machine.</p><p>Let us compare two projects that use the same technique. One designs recommendation algorithms so that minors spend as much time as possible in front of the screen, because that way more advertisements are sold. The other uses similar algorithms to detect diseases in medical images and make diagnosis cheaper and more accessible. The mathematics is almost the same; the ethical sense is the opposite.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: who should decide what is researched: scientists, the companies that pay, states, or citizens? What risk would there be in leaving it only in the hands of each one of them?</p>\n<p>That distinction is only the way in. Stoicism, which Zeno of Citium began teaching around 300 BC in a painted porch in Athens (the <em>Stoà poikílē</em>, hence its name), is also a <strong>virtue ethics</strong>: for the Stoics, the only true good is having an excellent character, and everything else (health, money, success) is <strong>indifferent</strong>. Not because it makes no difference whether we have it, but because it does not make us better or worse people: it is preferable to be healthy, but a sick person can live well and a healthy one badly. That excellence has four faces: <strong>practical wisdom</strong> (<em>phrónēsis</em>), which knows what should be done and guides the others; <strong>courage</strong> (<em>andreía</em>), which endures pain and fear; <strong>justice</strong> (<em>dikaiosýnē</em>), which treats others as what they are, part of a single human community; and <strong>temperance</strong> (<em>sōphrosýnē</em>), which is content with enough.</p>\n<p>The most original thing about the Stoics is their idea of the <strong>emotions</strong>. Chrysippus held that a passion is a <strong>judgement</strong>, and a mistaken one at that: anger, for example, is believing that someone has done me an unjust harm and deserves to pay for it. If I change the judgement, the emotion changes. Seneca, in <em>On Anger</em>, adds a nuance: there are reactions we do not choose, such as blushing or jumping at a clap of thunder, and they are not yet passions; the passion begins when we give our assent to that first impression and turn it into a judgement. That is why the goal, <em>apátheia</em>, is not coldness but not letting oneself be carried away: the Stoic sage also knows good emotions, such as serene joy (<em>chará</em>) or caution in the face of a real danger. It is the same question you saw in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t2\">Topic 2</a>: are emotions judgements or reactions of the body?</p>\n<p>For the Stoics, philosophy is practised every day, like training. Marcus Aurelius wrote his <em>Meditations</em> every night to review what he had done; Seneca advised his friend Lucilius to imagine possible misfortunes in advance (losing his job, an illness) so that they would not catch him unprepared. In the twentieth century, the psychologists Albert Ellis and Aaron Beck acknowledged this heritage when they created cognitive therapies, which treat anxiety and depression by teaching people to detect and correct the interpretations they make of what happens to them. Ellis often quoted a saying of Epictetus: what disturbs us is not things, but the opinions we hold about them.</p>\n<p>Stoicism has also been criticised. The first criticism concerns its foundation: the Stoics believed in a cosmos governed by a divine reason (<em>lógos</em>) that orders everything for the good, and so accepting what happens meant cooperating with that order; anyone who does not share this picture of the universe has to look for another reason to accept. The second is that sorting things into two piles, the controllable and the uncontrollable, is too clear-cut: an exam mark does not depend only on me, but it is not unrelated to how much I study either; William Irvine (<em>A Guide to the Good Life</em>, 2009) therefore proposes a third box, for what we partly control. The third is political: if the injustice I suffer does not depend on me, should I accept it calmly? Its defenders point out that justice is among the four virtues, that the Stoics saw themselves as citizens of the world and that Marcus Aurelius governed an empire; what would not be Stoic is a self-help version that keeps the calm and forgets about other people.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: faced with failing an exam, a traffic jam or an illness, which part depends on you and which does not? Does it really help to focus only on the first, or are there things we should not simply accept? You will study the Stoics in depth in Year 2, in Hellenistic philosophy; you can place them in the <a href=\"#genealogias/vida\">“Happiness and the good life”</a> line in Genealogies.</p>\n<h3>Utilitarianism (Bentham and Mill)</h3>\n<p><strong>Utilitarianism</strong> applies the criterion of pleasure to society: the good action is the one that produces ‘<strong>the greatest happiness of the greatest number</strong>’ (<strong>Bentham</strong>). <strong>John Stuart Mill</strong> adds that there are higher pleasures (those of the intellect and the feelings): ‘it is better to be a human being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied’.</p>\n<p>Why is judging by consequences so attractive? For two reasons. The first is that good intentions are not enough: someone who wants to help and makes everything worse has not acted well, and the proverb puts it harshly: the road to hell is paved with good intentions. The second is that consequences can be observed and compared, whereas intentions are inside each person: that is why governments, which make decisions for millions of people, tend to reason in terms of results.</p>\n<p>Utilitarianism counts the happiness of everyone with the <strong>same weight</strong>, including that of the agent themselves: that is its way of being <strong>impartial</strong>. But it meets a classic objection: if only the balance of well-being matters, it could justify punishing an innocent person or sacrificing a minority when that benefits the majority. In response, a distinction is drawn between <strong>act utilitarianism</strong> (the utility of each particular action is calculated) and <strong>rule utilitarianism</strong> (norms are judged —respecting rights, not lying…— because their general observance produces more well-being than their exceptions).</p>\n<p>Utilitarianism is only one of the <strong>consequentialist ethics</strong>, those that judge an action by its results. They differ in <em>whom</em> they take into account: <strong>ethical egoism</strong> asks us to seek only our own good; <strong>ethical altruism</strong>, only that of others; and <strong>utilitarianism</strong>, that of everyone affected, oneself included. The first two share a problem: they give special weight to one person, or take it away, without a reason to justify it. In Year 2 you will see this in more detail in the topic <a href=\"#teoria/hf-etica-deber\">‘Ethics of happiness and ethics of duty: Kant versus utilitarianism’</a>.</p>\n<p>An <strong>institution</strong> is a stable social agreement on values and norms: school, health care, business. An <strong>organisation</strong> is its concrete form: this institute, that hospital, that factory. And an organisation can betray the institution it claims to embody, like a company that deceives its customers. A person does not need to justify that they exist; an institution does. It legitimises itself by providing goods to society, and if it does not do so, it can be demanded and trust withdrawn. Peter Drucker, in <em>Post-Capitalist Society</em> (1993), described ours as a society of organisations: almost everything we do passes through them.</p><p>There are three views of the company. The <strong>unitary</strong> one sees it as a matter for the shareholders; the <strong>dual</strong> one, as a conflict between owners and workers; the <strong>plural</strong> one, as cooperation among all interest groups. From the plural perspective, a company has a moral character and forges it through its daily decisions. Norms are not enough: it needs values such as honesty, justice, respect, transparency or sustainability, defined together with those affected. Corporate social responsibility is voluntary, but neither optional nor decorative. It includes ecological responsibility, which is difficult because the costs arrive far away and late: acid rain falls hundreds of kilometres from the chimney, and the damage to forests and lakes takes years to become noticeable.</p><p>Against cosmeticism there are tools: ethical codes, reports, external audits and ethics committees. And there is Kant's principle of publicity (<em>Perpetual Peace</em>, appendix II): an action that affects others is unjust if its maxim could not be made public without frustrating its purpose. A textile company with an impeccable ethical code and suppliers who exploit their seamstresses would not pass it.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: would publishing an ethical code be enough to be an ethical company? What would have to happen for you to believe it?</p>\n<details><summary><strong>Box: the ethics of Plato and Aristotle</strong></summary>\n<p>Before the great modern theories, Greek philosophy had already given two answers that are still with us.</p>\n<p><strong>Moral intellectualism (Socrates and Plato).</strong> For Socrates, <strong>no one does wrong knowingly</strong>: whoever does evil does so because they <strong>are ignorant</strong> of what is truly good. To know what the good is and to do it would thus be the same thing; wickedness is a form of ignorance. Plato takes this up and ties it to his theory of the <strong>Forms</strong>: reason <strong>discovers</strong> the Good, it does not invent it, and the just life is that of the well-ordered soul, in which reason guides the spirit and the desires.</p>\n<p><strong>Virtue as a mean (Aristotle).</strong> For Aristotle, knowing what is good is not enough: virtue (<em>areté</em>) is a <strong>habit</strong> acquired by <strong>practice</strong>, as one learns to play an instrument. Moral virtue lies in the <strong>mean</strong> between two extremes —courage, between cowardice and recklessness; generosity, between meanness and wastefulness—, a mean that is not the same for everyone and that <strong>practical wisdom</strong> (<em>phrónesis</em>) fixes. Its end is <strong>eudaimonia</strong>, a life that goes well as a whole, not a passing pleasure.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> Aristotle’s ‘mean’ is not an <strong>arithmetical average</strong> nor ‘always staying halfway’. Faced with a serious injustice, full indignation may be the right thing: the mean is the <strong>fitting</strong> point according to the situation, not a lukewarm middle. And against intellectualism the objection has been raised since antiquity (by Aristotle himself) of <strong>weakness of will</strong>: sometimes we know what is right and still do not do it.</p>\n</details>\n<p><strong>The four cardinal virtues.</strong> Greek ethics arranged the virtues around four main ones, which Plato already lists in the <em>Republic</em> and which Christianity later inherited under the name of <strong>cardinal</strong> virtues (from <em>cardo</em>, the hinge on which a door turns):</p><ul><li><strong>Courage</strong> (<em>andreía</em>): overcoming fear, not out of recklessness, but because reason shows that it is worth it. Defending someone from an attack is courage; taking risks to show off is not.</li><li><strong>Justice</strong> (<em>dikaiosýnē</em>): respecting others and giving each their due. Aristotle thought it the most complete, because it always looks to the good of others. Keeping your word to a friend is justice, and so is seeking laws that treat everyone alike.</li><li><strong>Prudence</strong> (<em>phrónēsis</em>): knowing how to deliberate and decide well in each concrete situation; it is not theoretical wisdom but good practical judgement. Managing money without squandering or hoarding it is prudence.</li><li><strong>Temperance</strong> (<em>sōphrosýnē</em>): governing desires and responding to pleasures in moderation, whether in food, drink or relationships.</li></ul><p>What matters is that these virtues <strong>go together</strong>: courage without prudence turns into recklessness; without temperance, desires drag reason along; and without justice, what one does well does not serve the community. That is why Aristotle holds that nobody can fully have one virtue without the prudence that guides them all.</p>\n<p><strong>Socrates: no one does wrong knowingly.</strong> In Plato’s <em>Protagoras</em>, Socrates defends a thesis that sounds strange: “no one goes willingly towards what is bad, or towards what he believes to be bad; it does not seem to be in human nature to want to go towards what one judges bad instead of towards the good” (Plato, <em>Protagoras</em> 358c-d, own translation). Behind it lies a simple line of reasoning. We all want what seems good for us; nobody wants to ruin their life. If someone acts badly, it is because they are mistaken about what suits them: they take for good what is not. That is why this position is called <strong>moral intellectualism</strong>: virtue is a kind of knowledge, and vice a kind of ignorance.</p><p>Three consequences follow, and Socrates accepted them. The first is that virtue <strong>can be taught</strong>, like any other knowledge. The second is that whoever acts badly needs, above all, to be freed from their error, which is why Socrates spent his days asking questions in the square of Athens. The third, the hardest: whoever truly knows what is good cannot fail to do it. Think of someone who smokes “knowing” that it harms them. Socrates would say that they do not fully know it: at the moment of lighting the cigarette, the nearby pleasure seems greater than the distant harm, just as a nearby object looks bigger than a faraway one. That is why, in the same dialogue, he proposes an <strong>art of measuring</strong> pleasures and pains, to correct the illusions that distance creates.</p>\n<p><strong>Plato: justice as harmony of the soul.</strong> In Book IV of the <em>Republic</em>, Plato explains that in each of us there are three forces that often pull in different directions. <strong>Reason</strong> (<em>logistikón</em>) calculates and seeks the truth; <strong>spirit</strong> (<em>thymoeidés</em>) is the energy of indignation, pride and courage; <strong>appetite</strong> (<em>epithymētikón</em>) asks for food, drink, pleasure and money. Plato observes that someone can be thirsty and, at the same time, not want to drink, because they know the water is contaminated: if one part pushes and another holds back, they must be different parts. Each has its own virtue: reason, <strong>wisdom</strong>; spirit, <strong>courage</strong>; the agreement of all the parts about which should rule, <strong>temperance</strong>. And <strong>justice</strong> is the order of the whole: each part doing its own work.</p><p>The text puts it like this: the just person “does not let each part of his soul do what belongs to another, but puts in order what is his own, rules himself, arranges himself, becomes his own friend and tunes the three parts together like the three notes of a harmony” (<em>Republic</em> IV 443d, own translation). Justice, then, lies first not in what we do to others but within: a soul at war with itself ends up acting badly outwardly too. Plato then transfers the scheme to the city, with rulers, guardians and producers. A question for today: when you decide something in the heat of the moment, who is in charge inside you?</p>\n<p><strong>Aristotle: what is proper to human beings?</strong> The <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> begins by observing that everything we do aims at some good, and that some ends are sought for the sake of others: we study to pass, we pass to get a qualification, we get a qualification in order to work… If the chain never ended, our desire would be empty; there must be an end wanted for its own sake and never for the sake of anything else. Everyone gives it the same name, <em>eudaimonía</em>, but they disagree about what it consists in. Aristotle reviews the most common ways of life (Book I, ch. 5): the life of <strong>pleasure</strong>, which is fit for cattle; the life of <strong>honours</strong>, which depends more on those who grant them than on oneself; the life of <strong>money</strong> will not do either, because wealth is only a means. The <strong>contemplative</strong> life he leaves for the end of the work.</p><p>To decide, he proposes the argument of the <strong>proper function</strong> (<em>érgon</em>, Book I, ch. 7). Just as a flute player or a sculptor is good if they do their job well, a human being will be good if they do theirs well. What is it? Not living and growing, which we share with plants, nor perceiving, which we share with animals, but acting in accordance with reason. Hence his definition: the human good is an activity of the soul in accordance with virtue, and moreover “in a complete life; for a single swallow does not bring the spring, nor does a single day, and so neither one day nor a short time makes anyone blessed and happy” (<em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> I 7, 1098a18-20, own translation). Happiness is not a mood but a way of living, judged over the years.</p>\n<p><strong>Two kinds of virtue.</strong> Aristotle distinguishes in the soul one part that reasons and another that does not reason but can listen to reason, as a child heeds a father’s advice: the part of desires and emotions. That is why there are two kinds of virtue (<em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> I 13 and II 1). The <strong>intellectual virtues</strong>, or virtues of the understanding, perfect the rational part: scientific knowledge (<em>epistḗmē</em>), craft (<em>tékhnē</em>), practical wisdom (<em>phrónēsis</em>), intelligence (<em>noûs</em>) and wisdom (<em>sophía</em>). They are acquired mainly through teaching, and that is why they need experience and time. The <strong>ethical virtues</strong>, or virtues of character, such as courage, temperance or generosity, order desires and emotions, and they are born of habit: their very name, <em>ēthikḗ</em>, comes with a slight change from <em>éthos</em>, habit.</p><p>Aristotle explains it with the crafts: “we become builders by building and lyre players by playing the lyre; in the same way, we become just by doing just things, temperate by doing temperate things and brave by doing brave things” (<em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> II 1, 1103a33-b2, own translation). No ethical virtue comes to us by nature: nature gives the capacity, and practice turns it into character. Hence the importance of education from childhood, since it makes a great difference whether one gets used to one thing or another. The sign that a habit is truly ours is pleasure: the generous person enjoys giving, and someone who gives grudgingly is not yet fully generous (II 3). The two kinds of virtue need each other: without good character, the skill of finding means is mere cleverness (<em>deinótēs</em>), which serves the honest person and the cheat alike (VI 12).</p>\n<p><strong>The mean, case by case.</strong> In chapter 7 of Book II, Aristotle goes through the virtues one by one and, for each area of life, points out a vice of deficiency, the virtue and a vice of excess. Some extremes had no name in Greek, and he had to invent one:</p><ul><li>Facing <strong>fear</strong> and confidence: cowardice, <strong>courage</strong> and rashness.</li><li>Facing bodily <strong>pleasures</strong>: insensibility, <strong>temperance</strong> and self-indulgence.</li><li>In <strong>giving and spending</strong> money: meanness, <strong>generosity</strong> and extravagance.</li><li>Facing great <strong>honours</strong>: small-mindedness, <strong>magnanimity</strong> and vanity.</li><li>Facing <strong>anger</strong>: spiritlessness (not getting angry even when one should), <strong>good temper</strong> and irascibility.</li><li>In <strong>talking about oneself</strong>: false modesty, <strong>truthfulness</strong> and boastfulness.</li><li>In <strong>jokes</strong>: boorishness (never laughing), <strong>wit</strong> (<em>eutrapelía</em>) and buffoonery.</li><li>In everyday <strong>dealings</strong>: surliness, <strong>friendliness</strong> and flattery.</li></ul><p>Two practical pieces of advice complete the list (II 8-9). The mean is not equidistant from the two extremes: rashness is more like courage than cowardice is, and extravagance more like generosity than meanness is. That is why one should first move away from the extreme towards which one leans, as people straighten a bent stick by bending it the other way. If you keep quiet in class for fear of looking ridiculous, your danger is not rashness: practise speaking up.</p>\n<p><strong>Knowing and not doing: <em>akrasía</em>.</strong> Against Socrates, Aristotle takes seriously an experience we all know: you decide that this afternoon you will study and end up spending three hours on your phone, knowing you will regret it. The Greeks called this <em>akrasía</em>, lack of self-mastery (from <em>krátos</em>, strength), and Aristotle devotes Book VII of the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> to it. To bring order to the problem, he distinguishes four characters:</p><ol><li>The <strong>virtuous</strong> person desires what is fitting and does it without inner struggle.</li><li>The <strong>self-controlled</strong> person (<em>enkratḗs</em>) feels disordered desires, but reason overcomes them.</li><li>The <strong>weak-willed</strong> person (<em>akratḗs</em>) knows what is better, but desire carries them away.</li><li>The <strong>vicious</strong> person, who has the vice (<em>kakía</em>), acts badly without conflict, because they already judge good what is bad.</li></ol><p>How can one know something and not act on it? Aristotle replies that there are ways of having knowledge without using it: the weak-willed person is like someone asleep, mad or drunk, who can recite lines of Empedocles without understanding what they are saying (VII 3). They know the general rule, “I must not waste the afternoon”, but passion hides the particular case from them. In this, Socrates was not entirely wrong. The moral difference between the weak-willed and the vicious is great: the first repents and can reform; the second cannot. Aristotle compares it with illnesses: vice is like a chronic disease, and weakness of will like attacks that come and go (VII 8). Something to think about: what helps you more to move from weakness of will to self-control, knowing more or changing your habits?</p>\n<p><strong>Friendship, a part of happiness.</strong> Aristotle devotes to friendship (<em>philía</em>) two of the ten books of the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, Books VIII and IX: more space than to any virtue. He begins with a forthright sentence: “without friends no one would choose to live, even if he had all the other goods” (VIII 1, 1155a5-6, own translation). He then distinguishes three kinds, according to what one wants from the other (VIII 3). In friendship <strong>for utility</strong>, the other is loved because they are useful, like the classmate with whom one does a group project. In friendship <strong>for pleasure</strong>, because they are pleasant, like the friend from parties or video games. Both are legitimate but fragile: when the usefulness or the fun ends, so does the friendship. The third, friendship <strong>for virtue</strong>, wants the other’s good for what the other is, and so it lasts as long as both remain good.</p><p>This last kind is rare, because there are few such people and because it takes time: according to a proverb Aristotle quotes, you do not know anyone until you have eaten the proverbial salt together, that is, until you have shared many meals (VIII 3). The true friend is “another self” (IX 4): their good matters to me as my own does, and through them I know myself better. That is why friendship is not an ornament of the happy life but a part of it. Something to think about: on a social network you can have hundreds of followers. How many of your friends would be friends for virtue, in Aristotle’s sense?</p>\n<p><strong>Is virtue enough to be happy?</strong> Here Aristotle parts company with Socrates and, later, with the Stoics. Happiness is virtuous activity, but that activity needs means: it is impossible, or at least difficult, to act well without resources, because many actions are done through friends, wealth or political influence. And some lacks tarnish happiness: someone very ugly, of low birth or living alone and childless would not be entirely happy, still less someone who lost their children or good friends (<em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> I 8, 1099a31-b6). Happiness therefore has a part that does not depend on us: <strong>fortune</strong>.</p><p>His example is Priam, the king of Troy, who in old age saw his sons die and his city fall (I 9-10). No one would call happy a life that ends like that. Even so, Aristotle adds a nuance: the virtuous person bears misfortunes with dignity, as a good general makes the best use of the army he has or a good shoemaker makes the best shoe out of the leather he is given; that is why he will never be wretched, though not fully blessed either (I 10, 1101a1-8). In the twentieth century, Bernard Williams and Thomas Nagel (both in 1976) called this problem <strong>moral luck</strong>, and Martha Nussbaum studied it in <em>The Fragility of Goodness</em> (1986). Something to think about: is it fair that happiness should depend partly on luck? Or is that precisely what makes it human?</p>\n<h3>Is it enough to be a good person? Objections to virtue ethics</h3>\n<p>Since Elizabeth Anscombe (1958) and Alasdair MacIntyre (1981) revived it, virtue ethics has once again become one of the major theories, and for that reason serious objections have also been raised against it. The most frequent is that <strong>it does not say what to do</strong>: faced with a difficult decision, ‘act as a virtuous person would’ seems an empty answer if I do not know what that person would do. Rosalind Hursthouse (<em>On Virtue Ethics</em>, 1999) replies that each virtue and each vice already gives a guideline (be honest, do not be cruel, do not be a coward) and that the other theories also need judgement to be applied: Kant’s imperative does not by itself say which maxim is mine. The second objection is the <strong>clash between virtues</strong>: a friend shows you a song they have written and you think it is bad; honesty asks you to tell them, kindness asks you not to hurt them. The Aristotelian answer is practical wisdom, which does not apply rules but looks, in each case, for the way to be honest without being cruel.</p>\n<p>Two other objections go deeper. One is <strong>cultural relativity</strong>: the virtues admired by an Athenian aristocrat, such as the magnificence of someone who pays for public festivals, are not those of a medieval monk or ours, so perhaps each society calls virtue whatever suits it. Martha Nussbaum (1988) replies that, although the names change, every culture has to respond to the same experiences (the fear of death, the sharing of goods, dealing with pleasures), and that virtue is the right way of facing each of them. The other is <strong>situationism</strong>, which comes from psychology: in a 1972 experiment, Alice Isen and Paula Levin found that people who had just found a coin in a telephone box were much more likely to help pick up some dropped papers than those who had not; and in the 1960s, Stanley Milgram got ordinary people to give what they believed were dangerous electric shocks to a stranger because an authority figure asked them to. Philosophers such as Gilbert Harman (1999) and John Doris (2002) concluded that stable character may not exist and that it is the situation that moves us. Defenders of virtue reply that these experiments show exactly what Aristotle already knew: that full virtue is rare and difficult, not that it is impossible.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: think of someone you admire for who they are, not for what they have achieved. Could you say what that person would do in your place when faced with a specific dilemma? And do you think they would behave in the same way if they were in a hurry, tired or surrounded by people doing the opposite?</p>\n<p>Alongside these schools there was a more radical one: <strong>cynicism</strong>. Its founder, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/antistenes\">Antisthenes</a></strong> (c. 445-c. 365 BC), a disciple of Socrates, held that happiness lies in <strong>virtue</strong> and in being self-sufficient (<em>autarky</em>), not in goods or fame. His most famous heir, <strong>Diogenes of Sinope</strong>, took it to the extreme, living in a barrel and despising conventions. And <strong><a href=\"#biografias/crates\">Crates of Thebes</a></strong> (c. 365-c. 285 BC) gave away his fortune to live with just enough; he was, moreover, the teacher of Zeno, the founder of Stoicism. The cynics provoked in order to recall something serious: that we need far less than we think.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: living with the bare minimum and despising what others pursue, is that freedom or just another way of drawing attention? How much of what you desire do you really desire? You will meet the cynics in Year 2, in Hellenistic philosophy.</p>\n<h2>5. Kant’s formal ethics</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t5-29\"><strong>Immanuel Kant</strong> criticises material ethics: if the good depends on an end (happiness), morality becomes relative and conditional. He proposes a <strong>formal</strong> and <strong>autonomous</strong> ethics, based on <strong>duty</strong>: an action is moral not because of its consequences, but when it is done <strong>out of respect for the moral law</strong>. That law is expressed in the <strong>categorical imperative</strong>, an unconditional command that reason gives itself:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>‘Act only according to that maxim whereby you can at the same time will that it should become a <strong>universal law</strong>.’</li>\n<li>‘Act in such a way that you treat <strong>humanity</strong>, whether in your own person or in the person of any other, always as an <strong>end</strong> and never merely as a <strong>means</strong>.’</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Kant distinguishes two ways of doing what is right. A shopkeeper who charges the fair price to a child who would not know if he were being cheated acts <strong>in accordance with duty</strong>; but if he does it only because it suits him to have a reputation for honesty, his action has no moral worth. It only has it when he acts <strong>from duty</strong>, because it is what ought to be done, even if nobody were ever to find out. That is why, for Kant, what makes an action good cannot be seen from outside: it lies in the maxim on which it is done.</p>\n<p>Ethics of duty also share a structure that separates them from utilitarianism. Their norms are not goals to be achieved as many times as possible, but <strong>limits</strong> on what each person may do. The philosopher H. J. McCloskey (1957) illustrated this with a case: a judge could convict an innocent man to calm a crowd that would otherwise cause riots in which several people would die; counting lives seems to favour the conviction, but almost all of us feel that the judge cannot do it. Three features follow from this: the difference between <strong>doing</strong> harm and <strong>not preventing</strong> it matters; there are <strong>permissions</strong> to look after one’s own life without having to maximise everyone’s good; and some accept that, in the face of a catastrophe, the limit may give way. In Year 2 you will see this developed in the topic <a href=\"#teoria/hf-etica-deber\">‘Ethics of happiness and ethics of duty: Kant versus utilitarianism’</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>The good will.</strong> Kant opens the <em>Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals</em> (1785) with a blunt thesis: of everything we can imagine, only one thing is good without qualification, a <strong>good will</strong> (ch. I, Ak. IV 393; this is how the Academy edition is cited, by volume and page). Everything else is good only under certain conditions: friendliness, memory or composure are excellent qualities, but a charming con artist or a cool-headed armed robber is worse precisely for having them. A good will is not a vague intention (“I meant well”), but the firm decision to do what one ought, using every means within our power. And its value does not depend on the result: if a girl stands up for a classmate who is being bullied and fails to stop it, her action is worth no less than if she had succeeded. A good will, says Kant, shines by itself, like a jewel (Ak. IV 394). For Bentham, by contrast, an action that does not improve the balance of well-being has been of no use at all.</p>\n<p><strong>From duty and in accordance with duty: two more cases.</strong> Kant distinguishes actions <strong>contrary to duty</strong> (lying to escape a punishment), actions <strong>in accordance with duty</strong>, which do the right thing for some other motive, and actions done <strong>from duty</strong>, out of respect for the moral law, the only ones with moral worth. His subtlest examples are not about self-interest but about inclination (Ak. IV 397-398). Almost all of us look after our lives because we want to live; but if someone, crushed by misfortune, wishes to die and still preserves their life because it is their duty, their conduct has a worth that the first person’s lacks. And someone who helps because they enjoy it acts well and deserves esteem, but the test comes on the day when, worn out by their own problems, they no longer feel anything for others and help them all the same. Hence his definition: duty is the necessity of an action from respect for the law (Ak. IV 400). <strong>Trick case:</strong> Kant does not say it is wrong to enjoy helping, nor that we must do good grudgingly. He says that enjoyment is not what makes the action moral: the question is whether you would also do it without it.</p>\n<p><strong>Maxims and imperatives.</strong> Whenever we act, we follow a <strong>maxim</strong>: the personal rule behind what we do, even if we never say it aloud. “If I haven’t revised, I’ll copy when nobody’s looking” or “I will never laugh at anyone for their looks” are maxims. Kant does not give a list of good maxims, but a test that every maxim must pass; that is why his ethics is <strong>formal</strong>. That test is an <strong>imperative</strong>, and there are two kinds (Ak. IV 414-416). <strong>Hypothetical</strong> imperatives command something as a means to an end: “if you want to pass, study”. They bind only someone who wants that end; if I don’t care about passing, they no longer concern me. Some are rules of skill, like those of a recipe; others are counsels of prudence for being happy, such as “look after your friends”. The <strong>categorical</strong> imperative, by contrast, commands without conditions: it does not say “don’t lie if you want to keep your reputation”, but “don’t lie”, and it holds for any rational being, whatever they may want. Kant states it in three main ways which, in his view, say the same thing from different angles.</p>\n<p><strong>First formula: universal law, with a case.</strong> Kant examines this maxim: “when I need money, I will borrow it and promise to pay it back, even though I know I won’t be able to” (Ak. IV 422). The procedure has three steps: state the maxim honestly; imagine a world in which everyone followed it, as if it were a law of nature, and ask whether in that world it could still work. It could not: if everyone in difficulty made false promises, nobody would believe any promise, and promising would become impossible. The maxim destroys itself, and whoever follows it can only do so as an <strong>exception</strong> to a rule that needs everyone else to keep it. Careful: the argument does not say “it would be bad if everyone lied”, counting harms like a utilitarian, but that the maxim <strong>cannot be conceived</strong> as a law without contradiction. There is a second level (Ak. IV 423-424): never helping anyone can be conceived as a universal law, but nobody can <strong>will</strong> it, because we will all need help at some point. From the first kind of failure come strict duties (not making false promises); from the second, wide duties (helping), which leave room to decide when and how.</p>\n<p><strong>Second formula: humanity as an end.</strong> The same case can be seen through the formula of humanity (Ak. IV 429-430): whoever makes a false promise uses the other person as a mere instrument, getting a loan out of them that they would never give if they knew the truth. The key lies in the word “merely”. We use other people as means all the time: the bus driver serves me to get to school, and I serve him to earn his wages; there is nothing immoral in that, because we both know the deal and can accept it. What is immoral is treating someone <strong>merely</strong> as a means, in a way they could not accept if they knew: deception and coercion are the typical cases. That is why Kant separates what has a <strong>price</strong>, which can be exchanged for an equivalent, from what has <strong>dignity</strong>, which is above all price (Ak. IV 434-435): a mobile phone can be replaced by an identical one; a person cannot. A question for today: does a free app designed to keep you hooked and sell your data treat you as an end or merely as a means? And is accepting terms and conditions that nobody reads really giving your consent?</p>\n<p><strong>Third formula: autonomy and the kingdom of ends.</strong> The third formula asks us to think of the will of every rational being as <strong>universally legislating</strong> (Ak. IV 431): the moral law does not reach me from outside; I recognise it with my own reason, and in obeying it I obey myself. Kant completes it with the <strong>kingdom of ends</strong> (Ak. IV 433), a community in which everyone is at once author and addressee of the laws, like a class that agrees its own rules and keeps them because they are its own, not because someone is watching. From here comes his criticism of earlier ethics, which he calls <strong>heteronomous</strong> (Ak. IV 441-444). It is not only someone who obeys out of fear or habit who is heteronomous; so is someone who does good to be happy, to feel good about themselves or to earn a place in heaven, because then the norm depends on something desired, and if one stops desiring it, it stops binding. Two pupils who don’t copy do the same thing seen from outside; if one doesn’t copy because there are cameras and the other because copying means deceiving whoever trusts the mark, only the second acts autonomously.</p>\n<p><strong>The most famous criticism: the murderer at the door.</strong> In 1797 the writer Benjamin Constant objected that, if telling the truth were a duty without exceptions, we would have to tell it even to a murderer who asks whether our friend, whom he is pursuing, is hiding in our house. Kant replied that same year in <em>On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy</em>, and did not back down: “Truthfulness in statements that cannot be avoided is a formal duty of each person towards everyone, however great the harm that may follow from it for oneself or for another” (Ak. VIII 426, own translation). His reason: whoever lies becomes responsible for what comes after, and every lie damages the trust on which any word given depends. Almost all of us find this answer unacceptable, and it is the classic example of the <strong>rigorism</strong> Kant is reproached for. But not all Kantians follow him. Christine Korsgaard (1986) argued that the murderer is already using our sincerity as a means to his crime, and that the formula of humanity would allow us to deceive him to protect the innocent. Is it Kant who fails, or only one way of applying him?</p>\n<p><strong>Other objections, and a proposal.</strong> The second classic criticism is the <strong>conflict of duties</strong>. You have promised to pick up your little brother at eight and, on the way, you come across an injured person: if you help her, you break your promise; if you keep it, you leave her alone. If both duties were absolute, one would have to give way, and Kant offers little help in deciding which. The third, that of <strong>empty formalism</strong>, was raised by Hegel (<em>Elements of the Philosophy of Right</em>, 1820, § 135): a purely formal test provides no content, and with a little ingenuity almost any maxim can be worded so that it passes. The fourth is <strong>coldness</strong>: in some verses of 1796, Schiller mocked a scrupulous man who gladly helps his friends and fears, for that very reason, that he is not virtuous. The Scot W. D. Ross (<em>The Right and the Good</em>, 1930) proposed a way out that keeps the best of Kant: there are several <em>prima facie</em> duties (“at first sight”) —keeping promises, not harming, making amends, gratitude, coming to someone’s aid— that genuinely bind, but which must be weighed when they clash. In the example, helping the injured woman would weigh more, and there would remain the duty to explain yourself and apologise to your brother.</p>\n<p><strong>Was Kant criticising the real Aristotle?</strong> Kant rejects <strong>eudaimonist</strong> ethics, those that ground morality in happiness (<em>eudaimonía</em>), because they would turn virtue into a means of obtaining something one desires. Many scholars today think the criticism hits the mark against textbook versions of ancient ethics, which presented virtue as the road to a reward, but much less against Aristotle. For him, virtue is not an instrument: the brave person acts “for the sake of the noble” (<em>tou kaloû héneka</em>, <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> III, 7), and happiness is not a prize that comes afterwards but the virtuous life itself taken as a whole. Kant would then have partly knocked down a <strong>straw man</strong>, a version of the opponent weaker than the real one. Christine Korsgaard (1996) went so far as to argue that the two are closer than they seem, since both ask us to do good for its own sake. The deeper differences remain: Aristotle starts from human nature and its end, and gives a central role to well-educated emotions; Kant starts from reason and distrusts the inclinations. To remember it: Aristotle teaches above all <strong>how</strong> to live well; Kant, <strong>why</strong> we should act well even when it brings us nothing.</p>\n<h2>6. Emotivism and applied ethics</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t5-32\">In opposition to those who ground morality in reason, <strong>David Hume</strong> holds a position close to <strong>emotivism</strong> (more precisely, a moral sentimentalism): when we judge something to be good or bad we do not draw that conclusion by rational means, but rather express <strong>feelings</strong> of approval or disapproval. In his own words: “Reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions, and can never pretend to any other office than to serve and obey them” (<em>A Treatise of Human Nature</em>, Book II, Part 3, Section 3).</p>\n<p>In the twentieth century, A. J. Ayer (<em>Language, Truth and Logic</em>, 1936) radicalised this idea: saying ‘stealing is wrong’ does not describe anything, but expresses disapproval, as if we said ‘Stealing!’ in an angry tone. That is why it would be neither true nor false. But a moral judgement does not only express what I feel: it also tries to get others to feel and act in the same way. Charles Stevenson (1944) stressed this <strong>prescriptive</strong> side: saying ‘this is good’ is like saying ‘I approve of this; do so as well’.</p>\n<p><strong>The ethics of duty versus the ethics of sentiment.</strong> The dispute between Hume and Kant did not end in the 18th century: it is one of the great lines of ethics. For Kant, the foundation of morality is a law that reason gives itself, valid for every rational being; feelings are changeable and can ground nothing, except one that reason itself produces, <strong>respect</strong> for the law. For <strong>Hume</strong> (<em>A Treatise of Human Nature</em>, 1739-1740), on the other hand, reason alone never moves us to act: it informs us about facts and means, but the impulse and moral approval arise from <strong>sympathy</strong>, the capacity to feel with others. <strong>Arthur Schopenhauer</strong> went further in <em>On the Basis of Morality</em>, an essay that the Royal Danish Academy of Sciences refused to award a prize in 1840: Kantian duty struck him as theological morality in disguise, a command that only makes sense if someone is there to command and punish, and he held that the only genuine moral motive is <strong>compassion</strong>, feeling another’s pain as one’s own. Today, <strong>Martha Nussbaum</strong> (<em>Upheavals of Thought</em>, 2001) proposes a third way: emotions are not blind, but <strong>judgements of value</strong> about what matters to us, and so they can be educated and corrected. Indignation at an injustice or compassion for someone who suffers would be part of moral reason, not its enemy. A question for debate: would you trust more someone who helps you because it is their duty, or someone who helps you because they care about you?</p>\n<p><strong>Another contrast: Spinoza, an ethics without duty.</strong> A century before Kant, Baruch Spinoza wrote an <em>Ethics</em> (published in 1677, after his death) in which the key word is not “duty” but <strong>power</strong>. Every being strives to go on existing and to increase its strength; that striving is the <strong>conatus</strong> (III, proposition 6), and in human beings it is called desire. What increases our power of acting we experience as <strong>joy</strong>; what diminishes it, as <strong>sadness</strong> (III, definitions of the affects 2 and 3). Spinoza turns the usual order round: we do not desire something because it is good, but we call it good because we desire it (III, proposition 9, scholium). And virtue is not obedience but each person’s very power (IV, definition 8). Compared with Kant, there are three differences. The passions are not set aside but <strong>understood</strong>: an affect we understand no longer drags us along. Freedom is not a break with nature but knowledge of the causes of what we feel. And happiness is not the reward of virtue but virtue itself (V, proposition 42). Nor is it selfishness: since nothing is more useful to a human being than another human being guided by reason (IV, proposition 18, scholium), whoever truly seeks their own power also seeks that of others. Faced with Heinz’s dilemma, Spinoza would not ask what the law commands, but which action increases the common power. You will find his whole system in the exploration “Spinoza: philosophy as a system”.</p>\n<p>Today ethics is also concerned with concrete problems—<strong>applied ethics</strong>: bioethics, environmental ethics, the ethics of technology and artificial intelligence, global justice. The same questions reappear in all of them: which ends are worth pursuing? Which duties bind us? How should we treat others?</p>\n<p>Few recent proposals have caused as much discussion as the <strong>ethics of care</strong>. It arose from a debate with the psychologist <strong>Lawrence Kohlberg</strong>, for whom mature morality is reached at the end of three levels (pre-conventional, conventional and post-conventional), when decisions are made according to <strong>universal principles of justice</strong>. <strong>Carol Gilligan</strong> noticed that this scale, built by interviewing only boys, ranked lower anyone who, like Ane on finding out that her friend has cheated in an exam, asks herself first of all how to help her without breaking the trust between them. For Gilligan this is a different moral voice, not an inferior one, centred on <strong>relationships</strong>, <strong>responsibility</strong> and <strong>care</strong>. <strong>Joan Tronto</strong> adds that caring is not a matter for women, but a shared task that morality and politics must place at the centre.</p>\n<h2>7. Are there moral truths? Objectivism, relativism and subjectivism</h2>\n<p>Anyone who argues about whether it is fair for a footballer to earn a hundred times more than a nurse takes for granted that there is something to argue about. Metaethics examines that assumption: do there exist <strong>moral facts</strong> that make our judgements true or false?</p>\n<p><strong>Moral objectivism</strong> answers yes: there are moral truths that hold for everyone, whether or not a society accepts them, just as 7 × 8 = 56 does not depend on anyone’s opinion. For Plato, reason <strong>discovers</strong> the Good; it does not invent it. Another version, the <strong>voluntarism</strong> of medieval theologians such as William of Ockham, makes the good depend on God’s will; against it weighs a question that comes from Plato’s <em>Euthyphro</em>: is something good because God wills it, or does God will it because it is good?</p>\n<p><strong>Subjectivism</strong> claims that moral truth depends on each person: ‘it is wrong’ would mean ‘I disapprove of it’. <strong>Cultural relativism</strong> transfers that dependence to the group: what each society approves is right, and there is no criterion above cultures. The sociologist William Graham Sumner gave it its modern form in <em>Folkways</em> (1906), and it was developed by anthropologists of Franz Boas’s circle, who wanted to curb European contempt for other peoples. Its intention is respectable, but it has serious problems:</p>\n<p><strong>A text: Greeks and Callatiae.</strong> The historian Herodotus (5th century BC) tells of a scene that has become the classic example of relativism:</p><blockquote><p>Darius, when he was king, summoned the Greeks who were with him and asked them for how much money they would agree to eat their dead fathers. They answered that they would not do it for anything in the world. Then he summoned some Indians called Callatiae, who eat their parents, and asked them, in front of the Greeks and through an interpreter, for how much they would agree to burn them on a pyre. They cried out loudly and begged him not to say such things. — Herodotus, <em>Histories</em>, III, 38 (own translation)</p></blockquote><p>Herodotus draws a lesson from this: if all peoples were asked to choose the best customs in the world, each, after examining them, would choose its own. That is why he agrees with the poet Pindar, who called custom (<em>nómos</em>) king of all things. Notice that the text does not say that all customs are equally valid: it records a fact, that each people sees its own as the only acceptable ones. That is descriptive relativism. And the context adds a nuance: Herodotus tells the story in order to condemn the Persian king Cambyses, who mocked the rites of the Egyptians and desecrated their temples. The conclusion he draws is therefore a moral one: it is madness to scorn what others hold sacred.</p>\n<p><strong>From the Sophists to Montaigne.</strong> This debate goes back a long way. Already in fifth-century BC Athens, the Sophists, itinerant teachers who travelled around different cities, had realised that norms vary from place to place, and they distinguished what exists by <strong>nature</strong> (<em>phýsis</em>), identical everywhere, from what exists by <strong>convention</strong> (<em>nómos</em>), established by each city. Protagoras held that “man is the measure of all things” (fr. DK 80 B 1). If what is just depends on agreement, each city possesses its own justice and none is truer than another. Against this, Socrates and Plato argued that every norm must rest on arguments and that it is possible to inquire into what justice itself consists in, over and above what each city agrees. Many centuries later, Michel de Montaigne stated in “Of Cannibals” that “each person brands as barbarous whatever is not part of his own customs” (<em>Essays</em>, I, 31). He is often interpreted as a relativist, but this needs to be qualified. Montaigne was a sceptic, who suspends judgement when there are insufficient grounds to pronounce. Despite this, he does make value judgements: in that same text he argues that Europeans who, under the pretext of faith, tear living people apart with tortures are more barbarous than the peoples who devour their enemies once they are dead. Suspecting our own prejudices does not imply abandoning all criticism.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nobody could criticise another society</strong>: neither ancient slavery nor the persecution of dissidents in a present-day dictatorship would be unjust, only ‘different’.</li>\n<li><strong>The reformer would always be wrong</strong>: those who fought against slavery or for women’s right to vote went against the majority and therefore acted wrongly. There would be no <strong>moral progress</strong>, only change.</li>\n<li><strong>Which society counts?</strong> Each person belongs to several groups (family, country, religion) that often disagree.</li>\n<li><strong>It contradicts itself if it becomes a norm</strong>: to say ‘therefore we must tolerate all cultures’ is to defend a universal value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Do cultures really disagree so much?</strong> The main argument for cultural relativism starts from disagreement: cultures have different moralities, so there is no common moral truth. The American philosopher James Rachels (<em>The Elements of Moral Philosophy</em>, 1986) raised two objections to it. The first is logical: from the fact that people disagree it does not follow that nobody is right; there have also been peoples who believed the Earth was flat, and that does not turn the shape of the Earth into a mere opinion. The second looks at the facts: many differences lie not in values, but in beliefs about the world. Greeks and Callatiae wanted the same thing, to honour their dead fathers; they disagreed about what honours them. Rachels adds that there are values shared by any society that lasts: caring for children, not always lying to one another and not killing members of the group without reason. Without that, no life together would hold up. A defender of relativism can reply that these minimums are very few and that in almost everything else, from sexuality to punishment, the differences remain deep. The question remains open: how much of what divides us is value and how much is belief?</p>\n<p>These objections do not by themselves prove objectivism, which also has difficulties: how are those truths known? Why is there so much disagreement? A fourth route, <strong>non-cognitivism</strong>, denies that moral judgements are true or false: in the 20th century, A. J. Ayer interpreted them as expressions of emotion, in the line of Hume.</p>\n<p><strong>Moral universalism.</strong> In opposition to relativism, universalism holds that there are ethical principles that bind any person, whatever culture they belong to. In its <strong>classical</strong> formulation, it is reason that discovers them. This is the case with Thomas Aquinas's natural law, inscribed in the conscience of every human being, or with the Kantian moral law, valid for any rational being. During the second half of the last century, a less ambitious proposal gained ground: that of a <strong>universal minimum</strong>. It is not required that everyone share the same ideal of the good life; it is enough to have certain principles acceptable from any culture, such as respect for life, recognition of the freedom and dignity of all, and rejection of serious injustices. Each in its own way, this objective is pursued by the Universal Declaration approved by the UN in 1948, Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971), and the research of Thomas Pogge on the basic rights of those living in poverty. The discussion is not closed. In 1993, the Vienna Declaration proclaimed the universality and indivisibility of human rights, although it called for attention to what is specific to each people. That same year, in Bangkok, various Asian governments signed a document that called for interpreting them according to the history and values of each region. Some saw this as a defence of plurality; others, as a pretext to evade their fulfilment. This is the same notion of ‘minimums’ that you will study when you come to Adela Cortina (section 13).</p>\n<h3>Max Weber: reasoning about means and reasoning about ends</h3><p>The German sociologist Max Weber distinguished, in <em>Economy and Society</em> (posthumous work, 1922, ch. I, § 2), several types of social action. Two of them matter especially for ethics. The <strong>instrumentally rational action</strong>, or instrumental action, calculates which means lead most efficiently to an end and what each one costs. The <strong>value-rational action</strong> is guided by the conviction that something is worthwhile for its own sake, whether a duty, a cause or a faith, regardless of the result. Weber also added affective action, driven by emotions, and traditional action, driven by custom.</p><p>Think of two classmates choosing a degree. One compares career prospects, salaries and entry requirements, and chooses the one that ensures more employment: he reasons instrumentally. The other chooses Nursing because caring seems valuable to her, even though there are better-paid options: she reasons according to values. The same happens with food: you can choose a diet only for its effectiveness in performing well in sport, or become a vegetarian because you believe that it is not right to raise animals in certain conditions, even if it costs you more.</p><p>In the lecture ‘Science as a Vocation’ (1917), Weber spoke of the <strong>disenchantment of the world</strong>: science and technology have taught that, in principle, everything can be mastered through calculation, without mysterious forces. But science cannot tell us what we should want. Values clash with one another like warring gods, and each person has to choose. Instrumental reason grows, and ends are left without a common arbiter.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can reason decide ends or only means? If it only calculates means, with what do you choose what really matters to you: with feelings, with tradition or with something that still deserves to be called reason?</p>\n<p>‘Economy’ comes from the Greek <em>oikonomía</em>, the management of the household. In Book I of the <em>Politics</em>, Aristotle makes a distinction. <strong>Economy</strong> is the use of the resources necessary to live well, and it has a natural limit: nobody needs infinitely many shoes. <strong>Chrematistics</strong> (<em>khrēmatistikḗ</em>) is the art of making money, which can become unlimited when accumulation becomes an end in itself. That boundless striving is for Aristotle a vice, because it confuses the means with the end and forgets that wealth serves to live well with others.</p><p>Centuries later, Adam Smith, professor of moral philosophy, published <em>The Theory of Moral Sentiments</em> (1759) before <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> (1776). He is remembered for holding that, in the market, the interest of each can benefit all. But he did not separate economics and ethics: private interest must operate within justice, without which no society can endure.</p><p>In Book V of the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, Aristotle analyses the forms of particular justice:</p><ul><li><strong>Distributive justice</strong> distributes common goods and burdens according to each person’s merit, like a scholarship awarded on the basis of academic record.</li><li><strong>Justice in exchanges</strong>, which the medievals called <strong>commutative</strong>, requires that what is given and what is received have an equivalent value, like the corner shop that charges a fair price and does not take advantage of someone in a hurry.</li><li><strong>Corrective justice</strong> restores the equality broken by a harm, like a fine proportionate to the offence, neither symbolic nor excessive.</li></ul><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is merit always a fair criterion for distribution? If a scholarship is awarded solely on grades, what happens to someone who gets lower grades because they work in the afternoons?</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>What does moral truth depend on?</th><th>Main problem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Objectivism</td><td>Moral facts independent of opinion</td><td>How do we know them? Why so much disagreement?</td></tr><tr><td>Cultural relativism</td><td>What each society approves</td><td>Prevents criticising other cultures and talking about moral progress</td></tr><tr><td>Subjectivism</td><td>What each individual approves</td><td>There would be no real disagreements: each person would be speaking only about themselves</td></tr><tr><td>Non-cognitivism</td><td>Nothing: moral judgements express attitudes</td><td>Why do we reason in morality as if there were something true?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Key idea: that cultures disagree in morality is a fact; that none of them can be mistaken is a debatable thesis, which would make moral progress impossible.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Is there such a thing as moral progress? The case of slavery.</strong> For thousands of years, almost all the great civilisations accepted slavery. Aristotle defended it in the <em>Politics</em> (Book I) with the idea that some human beings are slaves “by nature”. In the Atlantic world, between the 16th and 19th centuries, millions of Africans were captured and sold on the other side of the ocean. Then, in little more than a century, slavery went from being normal to being a crime: the United Kingdom banned the slave trade in 1807 and slavery in its colonies in 1833; the United States abolished it in 1865, after a civil war; Spain, in Puerto Rico in 1873 and in Cuba in 1886; Brazil, in 1888. Mauritania was the last country to abolish it in law, in 1981. If cultural relativism were true, all this would have been merely a change of customs, and the abolitionists, who went against the majority of their time, would have acted wrongly. Hardly anyone believes that. Peter Singer (<em>The Expanding Circle</em>, 1981) proposed an explanation: reason pushes us to widen the circle of those who count, from the family to the tribe, from the tribe to the nation and from the nation to the whole of humanity. Progress would not be inevitable, since it can go backwards, but it would be recognisable: treating as equals those who were once excluded.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about:</strong> can we judge the past by today’s values? Some say it is not fair: Aristotle thought with the ideas of his time, and we, in his place, might have thought the same. Others reply that in every age there were people who saw the injustice — Aristotle himself reports that some already held that slavery is against nature, and in the 16th century Bartolomé de las Casas denounced the treatment of the indigenous peoples of the Americas — and that, if we could not judge the past, we could not say that we have improved either. Perhaps two questions should be kept apart: whether a practice was unjust, and whether the people of the time were to blame for not seeing it. A person can live inside an injustice without having invented it, and the injustice is still an injustice. Which practice of today do you think people will consider unjust a hundred years from now?</p>\n<p>Can one <em>define</em> what “good” is? The Englishman <strong><a href=\"#biografias/moore\">George Edward Moore</a></strong> (1873-1958) answered no, in his <em>Principia Ethica</em> (1903). According to Moore, whoever identifies “good” with a natural property —pleasure, usefulness, what evolution favours— commits the <strong>naturalistic fallacy</strong>. His proof is the <strong>open question</strong>: of any definition (“good is what gives pleasure”) it always makes sense to go on asking “but is what gives pleasure really good?”; if the question still stands, then “good” did not mean the same as “to give pleasure”. For Moore, “good” is a simple property that is grasped, but not defined. It is a close relative of <strong>Hume’s law</strong>: from the <em>is</em> the <em>ought</em> does not simply follow.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: if “good” cannot be defined by anything in the world, how do we know what is good? Do we intuit it, feel it or agree on it? You will meet Moore again in Year 2 in analytic philosophy, alongside Russell and the early Wittgenstein.</p>\n<h2>8. How is moral judgement formed? From Piaget to Gilligan</h2>\n<p><strong>Moral conscience.</strong> We call <strong>moral conscience</strong> the capacity to judge our own acts as good or bad. It does not merely register which norms hold around us: it works like an inner voice that guides us before we decide and afterwards acquits or accuses us. Socrates spoke of something similar. In the <em>Apology</em> (31c-d), Plato has him say that since childhood he has been accompanied by a kind of divine voice, his <em>daimónion</em>, which turns him away from what he is about to do but never urges him to do anything. Conscience is not born ready-made: <strong>it is formed</strong>. First we internalise the norms of those who bring us up, with their rewards and punishments; then we learn to examine them and, at best, to give ourselves our own reasons. That is why it can be mistaken. There is an <strong>erroneous</strong> conscience, which judges good what is bad, like that of someone who has grown up believing that certain people are worth less; a <strong>lax</strong> conscience, which excuses everything; and a <strong>scrupulous</strong> conscience, which sees guilt everywhere. Forming it well does not mean obeying more, but learning to judge better: with information, listening to those affected and being ready to change one’s mind. That is what the psychologists you will see next set out to study.</p>\n<p>The Swiss psychologist <strong>Jean Piaget</strong> (<em>The Moral Judgement of the Child</em>, 1932) told children pairs of stories: one boy accidentally breaks fifteen cups when opening a door; another breaks just one while sneaking some jam. The younger children blame the first more, because the damage is greater; the older ones attend to the <strong>intention</strong>. Piaget distinguished a <strong>heteronomous morality</strong>, with norms imposed by adults that seem sacred, and an <strong>autonomous morality</strong>, which appears around the age of nine or ten, with norms seen as agreements between equals that can be changed. In this way he takes up Kant’s pair of concepts.</p>\n<p><strong>Lawrence Kohlberg</strong> extended the scheme from his doctoral thesis (1958). He presented dilemmas and asked <strong>why</strong> people answered as they did. The most famous is the <strong>Heinz dilemma</strong>: his wife will die without a medicine that the pharmacist sells at ten times its cost; Heinz can only raise half and ends up stealing it. Should he have done so? What matters is the reason, not the ‘yes’ or the ‘no’.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><caption>What fixes the stage is the reason, not the verdict: stealing or not stealing can be defended from almost any stage.</caption><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>How someone might reason about the Heinz dilemma</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Pre-conventional level</th></tr><tr><td>1. Punishment and obedience</td><td>‘If he steals, they will catch him and he will end up behind bars.’</td></tr><tr><td>2. Individualism and exchange</td><td>‘Stealing is in his interest: without his wife, he is the one who loses out.’</td></tr><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Conventional level</th></tr><tr><td>3. Approval of those close to us (‘good boy’, ‘good girl’)</td><td>‘If he lets her die, his family will never forgive him.’</td></tr><tr><td>4. Maintaining the social order</td><td>‘I understand his anguish, but the law applies to everyone, him included.’</td></tr><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Post-conventional level</th></tr><tr><td>5. Social contract and individual rights</td><td>‘The right to life outweighs the right to property; a law that forgets this must be changed.’</td></tr><tr><td>6. Universal ethical principles</td><td>‘Every life has unconditional value: Heinz must save it and then accept the legal consequences.’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>For Kohlberg, the stages are gone through in order and without skipping, and most adults remain at the conventional level. Stage 6 hardly appeared in his data and was eventually withdrawn from his scoring system.</p>\n<p><strong>What Kohlberg meant by justice.</strong> For Kohlberg, each stage resolves conflicts better than the one before because it understands <strong>justice</strong> better, which he defined with two ideas: <strong>equality</strong> (everyone’s interests count the same) and <strong>reciprocity</strong> (what I ask for myself I must grant to others). At stage 6, these two ideas come together in the principle of <strong>reversibility</strong>: a decision is just if any of those involved would accept it were they to take the place of the others. Kohlberg called this the game of “moral musical chairs”: in the Heinz dilemma, one would have to stand in turn in the place of the wife, the pharmacist and Heinz himself, and ask what solution each would defend without knowing which role they will get. It is a version of the golden rule — do not do to others what you would not want done to you — and Kohlberg linked it to Rawls’s “veil of ignorance”. He also wanted to take his theory into schools. One of his students, Moshe Blatt, found that discussing dilemmas in class moved some of the pupils on to the next stage. And from 1974 onwards Kohlberg promoted “just communities”, schools in which pupils and teachers decided the rules together in an assembly, with one vote per person.</p>\n<p><strong>Carol Gilligan</strong> (<em>In a Different Voice</em>, 1982) started from the fact that Kohlberg’s first samples were only of males and that girls tended to score lower. She illustrated it with two eleven-year-olds: Jake solved the dilemma like a maths problem (life is worth more than money, so he must steal); Amy proposed talking to the pharmacist or asking for a loan, and feared that if Heinz went to prison his wife would be left alone. The scale saw Amy as less mature; Gilligan saw a different way of reasoning, attentive to <strong>relationships</strong>.</p>\n<p>The discussion is still open. Reviewing dozens of studies, Lawrence Walker (1984) found hardly any differences between men and women with the same education, and some critics warn that a supposed ‘female voice’ may reinforce stereotypes. What remains is that justice does not exhaust moral maturity. One objection affects the whole model: it collects <strong>arguments</strong> about imaginary dilemmas, and arguing well does not guarantee <strong>acting</strong> well. One can reason like a stage 5 in class and say nothing when someone is being mocked in the class WhatsApp group.</p>\n<p><strong>Two further criticisms of Kohlberg’s model.</strong> The first is <strong>cultural</strong>. In 1985 the psychologist John Snarey reviewed 45 studies carried out in 27 cultural areas: the early stages appeared everywhere, but the post-conventional ones almost only in cities and among educated people, above all in the West. Does that mean the rest of the world is less mature, or that the scale ranks one particular morality as superior, that of the individual with rights? In societies that give more weight to the community, the family or the sacred, someone can reason with great subtlety without using the language of rights. The second criticism comes from more recent psychology and calls into question the very role of reasoning. Jonathan Haidt told his participants stories in which nobody is harmed, such as that of a woman who cleans the toilet with an old flag of her country. Many condemned her without being able to explain what was wrong with it. For Haidt (2001), moral judgement is above all a quick <strong>intuition</strong>, and reasoning usually comes afterwards, like a lawyer defending what we have already decided. His critics reply that reasoning does change intuitions, albeit slowly, and that the history of moral progress proves it.</p>\n<p>Behind this discussion lies an earlier warning. In 1968 the psychologist Naomi Weisstein published an article with an ironic title, ‘Kinder, Küche, Kirche as Scientific Law’, in which she showed that many psychological theories about ‘the nature of woman’ were based on prejudice rather than data. Her criticism targets <strong>essentialism</strong>: attributing fixed traits to all women, or to all men, simply because they are women or men. That is why Gilligan is read carefully today: the voice of care would be a valuable way of reasoning, not a feminine essence.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: moral judgement matures by moving from obeying other people’s norms to reasoning with one’s own principles; Gilligan reminds us that the capacity to attend to others also matures.</strong></p>\n<p>To go further: try for yourself the case Kohlberg worked with, <a href=\"#dilemas/cla-heinz\">‘Heinz’s dilemma’</a>, before and after reading this section: what counts is not what you decide but the reasons you give. At the post-conventional level a possibility appears that tests the relationship between morality and law: <strong>civil disobedience</strong>, breaking in public and without violence a law that is considered unjust, while accepting the penalty. Henry David Thoreau defended it in 1849, and Gandhi and Martin Luther King practised it; its critics reply that if each person decides which laws to obey, the rule of law is weakened. The hints exercise <a href=\"#pistas/fil-legitimidad\">‘What makes power legitimate?’</a> goes through that debate.</p>\n<h2>9. Utilitarianism in depth: can happiness be calculated?</h2>\n<p><strong>Jeremy Bentham</strong> opens his <em>An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation</em> (1789) by stating that nature has placed us under the governance of two sovereign masters: <strong>pain</strong> and <strong>pleasure</strong>. If that is what moves us, it is enough to measure. His <strong>felicific calculus</strong> assesses each pleasure or pain by its intensity, duration, certainty, propinquity, fecundity (whether it brings more pleasures), purity (whether it brings no pains) and <strong>extent</strong> (how many people it reaches). Bentham was above all a reformer: he called for less cruel prisons and for not punishing conduct that harms nobody.</p>\n<p>It is worth reading how Bentham begins, because the whole theory fits into a few lines. Having named the two masters, pain and pleasure, he adds: “it is for them alone to point out what we ought to do, as well as to determine what we shall do”. And he immediately defines his principle: the <strong>principle of utility</strong> is the one that approves or disapproves of any action according to the tendency it appears to have to increase or diminish the happiness of the party whose interest is in question (<em>An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation</em>, 1789, ch. I, § 1-2). Notice two things. First, Bentham moves without a pause from what moves us to what we ought to do: since we do in fact seek pleasure, morality would consist in seeking it well. That leap from ‘is’ to ‘ought’ is precisely the one Hume had asked us to watch. Second, ‘the party whose interest is in question’ can be a person or a community, and Bentham warns that the community is a <em>fictitious body</em>: its interest is nothing but the sum of the interests of the individuals who make it up (§ 4). There is no good of the nation above real people; you have to count person by person.</p>\n<p>Let us try the calculus on a small case. It is Thursday night, you have a History exam tomorrow and some friends invite you to a concert. Bentham would ask you to score each option on his seven dimensions. The concert wins on <strong>intensity</strong> and <strong>propinquity</strong>: the pleasure is strong and immediate. It loses on <strong>duration</strong> (three hours) and <strong>certainty</strong> (the band may disappoint), and above all on <strong>purity</strong> and <strong>fecundity</strong>: it almost certainly brings a bad mark and a weekend in a bad mood. Studying gives a weak and distant pleasure, but a sure, lasting and fruitful one: passing opens the way to other pleasures, such as a summer without resits. That leaves <strong>extent</strong>: who else is affected? Your friends, who will have more fun with you, and your family, who will suffer a fail along with you. Once the sum is done, the answer may vary from one person to another, and Bentham would accept that: the calculus does not always give the same result; it gives a method for deciding. The criticisms come straight away. What number do you give to the thrill of a live song? And how does your disappointment compare with someone else’s joy? This <em>comparison between persons</em> is the most serious difficulty: without a common unit of happiness, the sum risks being an impression disguised as a calculation. Even so, governments do something similar when they compare the costs and benefits of a law or a road.</p>\n<p>Example: a town council hesitates between a sports centre and expanding home help for the elderly. The utilitarian does not ask who protests most, but which option produces the most total well-being, counting each person affected as one and nobody as more than one. <strong>Mill</strong>, in <em>Utilitarianism</em> (1863), added that the quality of pleasures is judged by those who know both; it has been objected that these ‘competent judges’ look very much like the cultured elite of his time.</p>\n<p>For Bentham there were no elevated pleasures as opposed to coarse ones. In <em>The Rationale of Reward</em> (1825, book III, ch. 1) he ventured to affirm that, if prejudices are set aside, <em>push-pin</em> (a children's pastime played with pins) has the same value as poetry or music as long as it provides identical pleasure; in our days it would be equivalent to saying that playing with one's phone for a while is worth as much as reading a novel. Mill, trained from a young age among Bentham's collaborators, thought that with that stance utilitarianism could not defend itself against critics who branded it a philosophy fit for pigs. He answered them in the second chapter of <em>Utilitarianism</em>, where the whole sentence you have already seen appears: “It is better to be a Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied. And if the fool or the pig think otherwise, it is because they only see their own side of the matter; the other side of the comparison knows both” (own translation). What Mill maintains is not that the pleasures of the intellect are more refined, but that anyone who has experienced both types chooses the superior ones, however much unease they may cost. Is that true? Mill himself admits that quite a few people, given the option, end up giving themselves over to the lower pleasures, and he clarifies this by means of a metaphor: the capacity for elevated feelings is like a fragile plant, which easily withers when it does not have time or opportunities. Think about it: do you know of anyone who, knowing both kinds of pleasure, has consciously stuck with the ‘inferior’ one? Would that confirm Bentham's stance?</p>\n<p>In the same chapter Mill answers another objection: before each action there is no time to calculate its effects on general happiness. His reply is a comparison. Nobody argues that navigation is not founded on astronomy because sailors cannot stop to calculate the Nautical Almanac: they go to sea with the calculations already done. In the same way, humanity has spent its whole history learning what actions tend to produce, and that experience is stored in ordinary moral rules, such as not lying or keeping promises, which Mill calls <strong>secondary principles</strong>. He adds that in the golden rule of Jesus of Nazareth, to do as you would be done by and to love your neighbour as yourself, lies the whole spirit of the ethics of utility. This is where <strong>rule utilitarianism</strong> starts, even though Mill did not formulate it under that name. J. J. C. Smart pointed out its weak spot in 1956: if, in a particular case, breaking the rule clearly produces more well-being, keeping it anyway would be a kind of <em>superstitious rule worship</em>. And if exceptions are added for such cases (‘do not lie, except when lying produces more happiness’), the rule ends up saying the same as act utilitarianism, as David Lyons argued in 1965. The rule utilitarian has to explain why a rule deserves respect even when its very reason for being, utility, advises breaking it.</p>\n<p>The classic objections use imaginary cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The trolley</strong> (Philippa Foot, 1967, developed by Judith Jarvis Thomson): a runaway trolley is about to run over five people and you can divert it towards just one. Almost everyone accepts this. But if you had to push a heavily built person off a bridge to stop it, almost everyone refuses, even though the balance is identical. If only consequences counted, there would be no difference.</li>\n<li><strong>The transplant</strong>: a surgeon could kill a healthy visitor in order to save five patients with his organs. Act utilitarianism seems to demand it; almost all of us see it as murder. The rule utilitarian answers that such a rule would destroy trust in hospitals.</li>\n<li><strong>The demandingness objection</strong>: Peter Singer (1972) compared letting distant people starve with not saving a child drowning in a pond so as not to ruin your clothes. If your luxuries could save lives, shouldn’t you give almost everything? For some this is the strength of the theory; for others, proof that it demands too much.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A deeper criticism does not dispute the cases but the way of adding up. John Rawls summed it up in one sentence: utilitarianism does not take seriously the <strong>distinction between persons</strong> (<em>A Theory of Justice</em>, 1971, § 5). Within a single life it is reasonable to accept a sacrifice for a greater good: you get up early today to pass tomorrow, because the one who suffers and the one who gains is you. Utilitarianism treats society as if it were one big person doing that same sum; but there the one who suffers and the one who gains are different people, and the pain of one is not made up for by the pleasure of another. T. M. Scanlon (<em>What We Owe to Each Other</em>, 1998) put it with a case. During a World Cup final, a technician, Jones, has an accident in the transmitter room: some equipment falls on him and he receives very painful electric shocks. To rescue him the broadcast must be cut for fifteen minutes, and millions of people are watching. If you add up the small annoyances of millions of viewers, perhaps they outweigh Jones’s suffering; even so, almost all of us think he must be rescued at once. Scanlon concludes that we should not add up, but compare each person’s complaint separately: Jones’s is far more serious than any viewer’s. Something to think about: from how many viewers onwards, if there is any such number, would your answer change?</p>\n<p>Bernard Williams (<em>Utilitarianism: For and Against</em>, 1973, written jointly with J. J. C. Smart) added the problem of <strong>integrity</strong>. Jim, a botanist on an expedition, arrives in a village where a captain is about to shoot twenty indigenous people as a warning. Since Jim is a visitor from abroad, the captain offers him a privilege: if he kills one, the other nineteen will go free; if not, all twenty will die. For the utilitarian the answer is obvious. Williams does not say the right answer is the opposite, but that the theory makes it too easy: it treats Jim as a channel through which the best sum passes and forgets that he would be the one who kills, while the captain would be responsible for the rest. If I am as responsible for what I let others do as for what I do, my projects and principles stop being mine. Close by is the objection from <strong>demandingness</strong>. Susan Wolf, in ‘Moral Saints’ (1982), doubted whether a life wholly devoted to maximising the good would be desirable: there would be no room left for playing the cello, reading novels or wasting an afternoon with friends. Apply it to your own life: if the money for the end-of-year school trip could pay for vaccines for hundreds of children, would going on the trip be a mistake? Utilitarians reply in several ways: some, that an exhausted person helps less and that we should distinguish what is obligatory from what is admirable; others accept outright that morality demands far more of us than we do.</p>\n<p>Behind the trolley lies a medieval doctrine, that of <strong>double effect</strong>. Thomas Aquinas outlined it when justifying self-defence (<em>Summa Theologiae</em> II-II, q. 64, a. 7): whoever defends themselves wants to save their own life, and the death of the attacker is a foreseen but unintended effect. Later manuals set it out in four conditions: that the act is not bad in itself; that the evil is not willed, only tolerated; that the evil is not the means of achieving the good; and that there is a proportionate reason. It explains why many people accept diverting the trolley but not pushing anyone off the bridge: in the second case, the death is the means. You can discuss it in the dilemma <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-tranvia\">‘The trolley and the bridge’</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: utilitarianism is impartial and useful for public decisions, but it clashes with the intuition that there are things that cannot be done to a person even if they improve the overall balance: that is the territory of rights.</strong></p>\n<p>In 1869 the Irish historian William Lecky described moral history as a <strong>widening circle</strong>: at first the affections reach only the family; then a class, a nation, a coalition of nations, the whole of humanity and, finally, our dealings with animals (<em>History of European Morals from Augustus to Charlemagne</em>). Peter Singer took the image as the title of <em>The Expanding Circle</em> (1981) and gave it a utilitarian basis: if what matters is the capacity to suffer and to enjoy, there is no reason why the edge of the circle should coincide with that of my town, my country or my species. His principle of <strong>equal consideration of interests</strong> (<em>Practical Ethics</em>, 1979) does not ask us to treat everyone the same, but to give the same weight to similar interests: a stranger’s pain counts as much as a friend’s, and a pig’s as much as a similar pain in a human being. Singer compares reason to an escalator: once you step on, it carries you on to widen the circle, even if you do not know how far. His critics accept that the circle has grown, but doubt that everything should enter it with the same weight: some think our obligations to those who depend on us are stronger, and that is exactly the point the ethics of care defends. Something to think about: where is the edge of your circle today? Who is left outside, and why?</p>\n<p>One last question for utilitarianism: is pleasure all that matters? Robert Nozick (1974) imagined an <strong>experience machine</strong> that, if you were plugged into it for your whole life, would make you live the most pleasurable experiences you wanted, without your knowing that they are not real. Most people would not plug in: we want to really do things and to be a certain way, not just to feel that we are. Hence two different versions of utilitarianism: the <strong>ideal</strong> utilitarianism of G. E. Moore, which also counts goods such as knowledge, beauty or friendship, and the <strong>preference</strong> utilitarianism of R. M. Hare and Peter Singer, which seeks to satisfy what each person truly prefers, whether or not it is pleasure.</p>\n<h2>10. Nietzsche’s suspicion: masters, slaves and the revaluation of values</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t5-60\"><strong>Friedrich Nietzsche</strong> does not ask what is good, but <strong>where</strong> our values come from and <strong>whom</strong> it suits that we hold them; that is why Paul Ricoeur placed him, with Marx and Freud, among the ‘masters of suspicion’. In <em>Beyond Good and Evil</em> (1886) and <em>On the Genealogy of Morality</em> (1887) he traces a <strong>genealogy</strong> of European morality.</p>\n<p>A country can grow year after year without progressing. Imagine a State whose gross domestic product (GDP) increases thanks to mining, while part of its population still lacks drinking water and the rivers become polluted. The figures say it is richer; the lives of many of its inhabitants say the opposite.</p><p>Amartya Sen, winner of the Nobel Prize in Economics in 1998, argued in <em>Development as Freedom</em> (1999) that development should be measured by people's real freedom. That is, by what they can truly be and do: feed themselves, educate themselves, receive medical care, participate in public life. Growth accompanied by poverty, inequality or environmental destruction is not moral progress. His ideas influenced the Human Development Index, which the United Nations has published since 1990 and which combines income, life expectancy and education.</p><p>Adela Cortina has brought this concern to everyday economics in <em>Business Ethics</em> (1994) and <em>For an Ethics of Consumption</em> (2002). For her, progress must be both technical and social. It is not enough to produce more: we must improve the material conditions of everyone and organise cooperation fairly, so as to move closer to freedom, justice, equality and peace. And there, not only governments and companies count: the consumer too. Choosing clothes made in workshops with decent conditions, even if they cost a little more, or asking where what we buy comes from is a way of saying what kind of economy we want.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what indicators would you use, besides GDP, to know if a country is progressing? And which ones to know if your town or neighbourhood is progressing?</p>\n<p>First there would have been a <strong>master morality</strong>: the strong called ‘good’ what they themselves were—proud, healthy, brave—and ‘bad’, in the sense of base, what was weak. It is a morality that <strong>affirms</strong> life. <strong>Slave morality</strong>, by contrast, is born of the <strong>resentment</strong> of those who cannot impose themselves: unable to take revenge through deeds, they take revenge through values. They call the strong ‘evil’ and turn their own weakness into merit: powerlessness passes for goodness and submission for humility. Nietzsche attributes this inversion to the Jewish priestly tradition and to Christianity, and sees its legacy in the modern morality of equality and compassion.</p>\n<p>Nietzsche does not present masters and slaves as two fixed groups of people, and he says so in the very passage where he introduces the pair: “Wandering through the many finer and coarser moralities that have ruled or still rule on earth, I found certain traits regularly recurring […]. There is master morality and slave morality; I add at once that in all higher and more mixed cultures there also appear attempts to reconcile the two moralities, even more often their confusion and mutual misunderstanding, and at times their harsh coexistence, even within the same human being, within a single soul” (<em>Beyond Good and Evil</em>, 1886, § 260; our own translation from the German). Masters and slaves are therefore <strong>types of valuation</strong>, two ways of deciding what has value, and they can fight within each of us. A few lines later Nietzsche gives the key to the first type: the noble kind of human being feels itself to be the one that <em>determines values</em> and needs nobody’s approval. The second type, by contrast, needs to look at the other in order to know what is good. Think of two ways of dressing: that of someone who wears what they like without wondering what others will think, and that of someone who dresses above all so as not to look like the people they despise. Both may end up in the same clothes; what changes is where the taste comes from.</p>\n<p>In <em>On the Genealogy of Morality</em> (1887, first essay, § 10), Nietzsche specifies what is special about this inversion: “The slave revolt in morality begins when ressentiment itself becomes creative and gives birth to values […]. Whereas all noble morality grows out of a triumphant saying yes to itself, slave morality says no from the outset to an outside, to an other, to a not-self; and this no is its creative deed” (our own translation). The difference lies in the order. The noble begins with himself, ‘I am good’, and only afterwards, almost in passing, calls bad whatever is not like him. Resentment begins with the enemy: first it needs someone to call wicked, and only afterwards, by contrast, does it declare itself good. Its morality is a <strong>reaction</strong> and cannot live without an opponent. A few pages later (§ 13), Nietzsche tells it as a fable. That lambs bear a grudge against great birds of prey surprises nobody; what is strange is that they turn it into a doctrine: “these birds are wicked, and whoever is as little like a bird of prey as possible, a lamb, should he not be good?”. For the reproach to work, he adds, we have to assume that the bird could choose not to be strong, as if behind strength there were a free subject who decides to use it. According to Nietzsche, it is this fiction of a choosing subject that makes blaming possible.</p>\n<p>The other side of resentment is <strong>herd morality</strong>. In <em>Beyond Good and Evil</em>, Nietzsche argues that when a community no longer fears enemies from outside, fear turns inwards and points at the individual who stands out: “everything that raises the individual above the herd and frightens the neighbour is henceforth called evil” (§ 201). And he concludes: “morality in Europe today is herd-animal morality” (§ 202; our own translation). This morality rewards whatever makes life comfortable and predictable: not drawing attention, being like everyone else, nobody suffering or being a nuisance. Its harshest portrait is that of the <strong>last man</strong>, in the prologue to <em>Thus Spoke Zarathustra</em> (1883): “No shepherd and one herd! Everyone wants the same, everyone is the same; whoever feels differently goes voluntarily into the madhouse”. And the last men, contented, say “we have invented happiness”, and blink (§ 5). Nietzsche’s target here is also utilitarianism: a morality that measures the good in well-being seemed to him the ideal of the herd. He summed it up with a jibe in <em>Twilight of the Idols</em> (1889): “Man does not strive for happiness; only the Englishman does” (‘Maxims and Arrows’, § 12). Something to think about: is Nietzsche right to see something mediocre in the pursuit of well-being, or is he confusing well-being with comfort?</p>\n<p>In the second and third essays of <em>On the Genealogy of Morality</em>, Nietzsche completes the story. <strong>Bad conscience</strong> is born when human beings begin to live in societies with laws: the aggressive instincts that can no longer be discharged outwards turn inwards, and the individual torments himself. Nietzsche points out that in German <em>Schuld</em> means both ‘guilt’ and ‘debt’: moral guilt would be an old debt that has been internalised. And this is where the <strong>ascetic priest</strong> comes in: in the face of suffering, which is what most drives to despair those who find no meaning in it, the priest offers an explanation (‘you are to blame for what you suffer’) that brings relief, but at the cost of turning human beings against their own life. In Year 2 you will see this in the topic <a href=\"#teoria/hf-sospecha\">on the philosophers of suspicion</a>, alongside Marx and Freud.</p>\n<p>Two more points. Slave morality does not triumph by being right, but through <strong>numbers</strong>: the weak far outnumber the strong, and their morality prevails as that of the herd. And it does not suppress the desire to dominate, but <strong>sublimates</strong> it, makes it spiritual: the resentful person still wants to defeat the strong, only now does so by judging them.</p>\n<p>An analogy of our own, not Nietzsche’s: Aesop’s fox, unable to reach the grapes, decides they were sour. The resentful person goes further: they call whoever has the grapes evil and not having them virtuous. How many criticisms on social media work like this?</p>\n<p>In <em>The Gay Science</em> (1882), a madman crosses the marketplace with a lantern in broad daylight shouting that ‘God is dead’. If inherited morality was a human creation and no faith supports it any longer, <strong>nihilism</strong> looms: nothing seems to be worth anything. For Nietzsche it is a crisis that must be passed through, not a destiny. The way out is the <strong>revaluation of all values</strong>, creating values that say ‘yes’ to life, a task he entrusts to the <strong>overman</strong>.</p>\n<p>Almost everything has been said about the <strong>overman</strong> (<em>Übermensch</em>), so it is best to start with the text. In the prologue to <em>Thus Spoke Zarathustra</em>, the protagonist comes down from the mountain and announces in the square: “I teach you the overman. Man is something that shall be overcome. What have you done to overcome him?” (§ 3). And shortly afterwards: “Man is a rope, tied between beast and overman, a rope over an abyss” (§ 4; our own translation). Three cautions help in reading it. First, Zarathustra does not describe anyone who exists, neither a biological type nor a people; he proposes a task, that of overcoming oneself, and the people in the square laugh at him. Second, the overman is not the one who rules over others, but the one who creates his own values after the death of God, without resentment or guilt; that is why Nietzsche links him to the <strong>eternal return</strong>, the test of whether you would want to live your life again, identical, an infinite number of times. Third, it is a poetic image, and interpreters disagree. Walter Kaufmann (1950) read it as an ideal of personal self-overcoming; others stress Nietzsche’s contempt for equality and fear it could serve to justify anyone who believes they are above the rules. The text lends itself to both readings, and they should be discussed with arguments, not legends.</p>\n<p>Fyodor Dostoevsky’s novel <em>Crime and Punishment</em> (1866) lets us put these readings to the test. Its protagonist, Raskolnikov, a poor student in St Petersburg, has written an article according to which humanity is divided into ordinary people, who must obey, and extraordinary ones, like Napoleon, who have the right to break the law if a great idea demands it. Before the crime he overhears another student in a tavern reasoning like a pub utilitarian: if an old pawnbroker were killed, her money could pay for a hundred good deeds; one death in exchange for a hundred lives, simple arithmetic. Raskolnikov kills the pawnbroker, and the novel tells of his collapse: fever, delirium and fear, until he confesses. Dostoevsky wrote before Nietzsche, but the book is often read as an early criticism of the ‘extraordinary human being’. On Nietzsche’s side, one could reply that Raskolnikov is not a free spirit but the opposite: he needs a theory to give him permission and a calculation to justify him, and that is why guilt crushes him. On Dostoevsky’s side, that guilt is not an illness to be overcome, but proof that conscience will not be won over by arithmetic. Nietzsche read Dostoevsky years later and said that he was the only psychologist from whom he had learnt anything (<em>Twilight of the Idols</em>, ‘Skirmishes of an Untimely Man’, § 45). Something to think about: who do you think is right about Raskolnikov’s guilt?</p>\n<p>The phrase appears in a scene from <em>The Gay Science</em> (§ 125): a madman lights a lantern in broad daylight, goes down to the marketplace and cries that he is looking for God. Those who do not believe laugh at him. Then the madman proclaims the death of God, of whom we are the murderers, and says that nobody has yet understood what this means. The mockery of the atheists is what matters: Nietzsche is not talking about a debate on the existence of God, but about what happens to a set of values when the faith that sustained them disappears.</p>\n<p>It is worth separating the author from his legend and reviewing the argument. The antisemitism attributed to him was his sister Elisabeth’s, with whom he clashed over it; she took control of his archive when he fell ill and, after his death (1900), launched <em>The Will to Power</em> as his great work, a selection of notes he never published. His disdain for equality, on the other hand, is genuinely his own. As for the argument, the genealogy is a poorly documented <strong>historical hypothesis</strong>, and the murky origin of a value does not prove that it is false (the <strong>genetic fallacy</strong>), something Nietzsche admitted when he distinguished where something comes from and what it serves for today. The greater difficulty is another one: if every value is a human invention, by what criterion does he declare master morality to be better?</p>\n<p>There is another objection, which comes from biology: morality would be much older than masters and slaves. Darwin already argued in <em>The Descent of Man</em> (1871) that the moral sense arises from the social instincts we share with other animals. The primatologist Frans de Waal (<em>Primates and Philosophers</em>, 2006) has documented in chimpanzees and bonobos reconciliation after a fight, consolation for the loser and the sharing of food. In a famous 2003 experiment with Sarah Brosnan, two capuchin monkeys performed the same task; when one received grapes and the other only cucumber, the latter rejected its reward, as if protesting against the injustice. If so, compassion and the sense of fairness would not be a late invention of the weak but an inheritance from evolution, although each culture then gives it its own form.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: Nietzsche does not propose another norm, but suspicion of received morality, above all that born of resentment, and the creation of values that affirm life.</strong></p>\n<h2>11. Ethics of care and applied ethics today</h2>\n<p>After Gilligan, the <strong>ethics of care</strong> became a theory in its own right. <strong>Nel Noddings</strong> (<em>Caring</em>, 1984) centred it on the relationship between the carer and the person cared for; <strong>Joan Tronto</strong> (<em>Moral Boundaries</em>, 1993) described it as a process—noticing a need, taking responsibility, caring competently and seeing how the other receives it—and brought it into politics. Their theses: we are all <strong>vulnerable</strong> and <strong>interdependent</strong>; the starting point is people in relationships, not isolated individuals; and empathy informs moral judgement instead of hindering it. A related question: who looks after dependent older people in your surroundings, and how much is that work paid?</p>\n<p><em>In a Different Voice</em> (1982) is often summed up as ‘boys reason with justice and girls with care’, but Gilligan denies this from the introduction onwards: the different voice she describes is characterised not by gender but by theme, and she associates it with women only because that is how she found it in her interviews. What she distinguishes are two orientations. The <strong>ethics of justice</strong>, she writes, starts from the premise of equality, that everyone should be treated the same; the <strong>ethics of care</strong>, from the premise of non-violence, that no one should be hurt (ch. 6). The first asks which rights clash and which weighs more; the second, which relationships are at stake and how to keep anyone from being left alone or hurt. The first sees the self as separate from others, and maturity as autonomy; the second sees it within a web of relationships, and maturity as the capacity to respond. Gilligan does not conclude that one voice is better, but that maturity needs both: care without justice can lead to limitless self-sacrifice, and justice without care to an indifferent impartiality. An example: two friends fall out and ask you to take sides. One way of looking asks who is right; the other, how to stay friends with both. Do you have to choose between them?</p>\n<p>Nel Noddings, a professor of education, gave the ethics of care its first systematic form in <em>Caring</em> (1984). She starts from a relationship between two: the one who cares and the one who is cared for. The one who cares does two things. First, they let themselves be absorbed by the other’s reality and receive it as the other lives it; Noddings calls this <strong>engrossment</strong> (<em>engrossment</em>). Then their energy shifts towards the other’s ends, in what she calls <strong>motivational displacement</strong>: for a while, what the other wants to achieve becomes what I want. And caring is not complete until the one who receives it responds, even if only with a smile or by carrying on. She also distinguishes natural caring, that of a mother who looks after her child without thinking, from ethical caring, the kind we do when it does not come naturally: we remember being cared for and think ‘I must’. For Noddings, morality is born of that memory, not of abstract principles. Her most controversial thesis follows from this: if caring needs a relationship that can be completed, my strong obligations reach those I can meet, and not in the same way distant strangers. Singer would say that this is exactly the prejudice that needs correcting. Noddings qualified her position in <em>Starting at Home</em> (2002), where she distinguishes caring for someone from caring about someone far away, which takes the form of supporting just institutions.</p>\n<p>Virginia Held (<em>The Ethics of Care</em>, 2006) took care from the home into politics. Modern theories, she says, picture society as a contract between independent adults who meet in the market; but nobody begins life like that: we have all been babies who would not have survived without care. That is why care is at once a <strong>practice</strong> (bathing, listening, keeping someone company) and a <strong>value</strong>. And it is more basic than justice: there can be care without justice, as in so many unjust families in which children have nonetheless been brought up, but no justice without care, because without it there would be no persons to respect. Held does not ask for justice to be replaced, but for the logic of the market not to be extended to everything: education, health or looking after the elderly are not done well if they count only as business. The debate remains open. Those who defend the primacy of justice warn of three risks in care: <em>partiality</em> (favouring my own over someone with a stronger claim), <em>paternalism</em> (deciding for others what they need) and the <em>exploitation</em> of carers, often women who forget about themselves. Those who defend care reply that a justice that pays no attention to real people is cold, and that perfect impartiality exists in no real life. Many today seek to combine them: justice to share out caring fairly, and care so that justice forgets no one.</p>\n<p>To each phase of the process Tronto matched a moral quality: <strong>attentiveness</strong> (noticing what someone else needs, something that haste and selfishness prevent), <strong>responsibility</strong> (taking charge, even when it is not a legal obligation), <strong>competence</strong> (caring well: good intentions are not enough if the care is poor) and <strong>responsiveness</strong> (listening to how the person receiving care experiences it, without deciding for them). In <em>Caring Democracy</em> (2013) she added a fifth phase, <strong>caring with</strong> others: that care should be shared out fairly in society, instead of almost always falling on the same people, often women and often badly paid.</p>\n<p><strong>Applied ethics</strong> takes theories to cases that divide society, and usually works with <strong>intermediate principles</strong> acceptable from different positions:</p>\n<ul data-bloque=\"fil-t5-69\">\n<li><strong>Bioethics</strong>. Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (<em>Principles of Biomedical Ethics</em>, 1979) propose four principles: <strong>autonomy</strong>, <strong>beneficence</strong>, <strong>non-maleficence</strong> and <strong>justice</strong>. In the Spanish euthanasia law of 2021, its defenders appeal to autonomy and to avoiding unbearable suffering; its critics, to protecting the vulnerable from possible pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Animal ethics</strong>. Bentham wrote that the question is not whether animals can reason or talk, but whether they can suffer. Peter Singer (<em>Animal Liberation</em>, 1975) popularised the term <strong>speciesism</strong>: discriminating by species as racism discriminates by race. Others accept duties towards animals without equating their interests with human ones. Cases: intensive farming, experimentation.</li>\n<li><strong>Ethics of artificial intelligence</strong>. Algorithms can inherit <strong>biases</strong> from their data: in 2016 a journalistic investigation reported that an American programme that estimated the risk of reoffending was wrong more often to the detriment of Black defendants (the company disputed this). Self-driving cars revive the trolley dilemma, and the question remains of who is responsible when a machine decides. In 2024 the European Union approved a regulation that classifies uses of AI according to their risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not every problem in a newspaper is a matter of applied ethics. For it to be one, there has to be <strong>reasonable disagreement</strong> between informed people acting in good faith, and looking at the law is not enough to settle it: something may be legal and morally debatable, such as paying a woman to carry a child in a country where it is allowed, or illegal and defensible, such as disobeying an unjust order. Three more fields show how it works.</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental ethics.</strong> The first question is to whom we owe consideration. For <strong>anthropocentrism</strong>, only to human beings: nature matters because we need it. The <strong>biocentrism</strong> of Paul Taylor (1986) extends it to every living being, and the <strong>ecocentrism</strong> of Aldo Leopold (<em>A Sand County Almanac</em>, 1949) to the whole community of soils, waters, plants and animals: an action is good if it protects the balance of that community. The second question looks at time. Hans Jonas (<em>The Imperative of Responsibility</em>, 1979) warned that modern technology can harm those who have not yet been born, and proposed a new imperative: act so that human life can go on existing on Earth. The Brundtland Report (1987) turned this into policy with the idea of <strong>sustainable development</strong>: meeting the needs of today without taking away from future generations the possibility of meeting theirs. The debate is how much can be asked of those who are poor today in order to protect those still to come.</p>\n<p><strong>Business ethics.</strong> In 1970 the economist Milton Friedman argued that the only social responsibility of a business is to make money within the law: sharing profits with social causes would mean spending money that belongs to the shareholders. Against him, R. Edward Freeman (1984) argued that a company is answerable to all the <strong>stakeholders</strong> it affects: workers, customers, suppliers, neighbours. The collapse of the Rana Plaza building in Bangladesh in 2013, where more than eleven hundred workers died in workshops sewing for European brands, raised the question of how far the responsibility of those who buy from a distant supplier extends. In Spain, Adela Cortina has defended a business ethics that is not reduced to advertising: a company also builds or destroys trust.</p>\n<p><strong>Social issues: distant poverty.</strong> Peter Singer (1972) put forward a case that has become famous: if you see a child drowning in a shallow pond, you must pull them out even if you ruin your clothes; and if so, you should also give part of your money to save children who are dying of hunger far away, because distance makes no moral difference. His critics reply from different sides: some say that helping is generous but not obligatory, and that nobody is obliged to sacrifice their own projects; others, such as Thomas Pogge (2002), say that the problem is not each person’s charity but rules of international trade that harm poor countries. It is a good example of how applied ethics puts theories to the test: Singer reasons as a utilitarian, and his critics with the ideas of duty, permission and justice that you have seen in this topic.</p>\n<p><strong>Animals.</strong> Besides the suffering Bentham spoke of, some argue that certain animals have <strong>intrinsic value</strong>, that is, that they matter in themselves and not only because of what they feel: in 1993 the Great Ape Project called for chimpanzees, bonobos, gorillas and orang-utans to be granted protection of their life and freedom, and for them not to be tortured. The specific questions are many: is intensive farming acceptable? And zoos? How far can experimentation go? Is there a difference between a pet and a farm animal? Those who reject this extension reply that rights go hand in hand with duties and responsibility, something only persons can have, although that does not authorise cruelty.</p>\n<p><strong>Bioethics: from abuses to principles.</strong> Bioethics was born above all out of scandals. At the Nuremberg trials, Nazi doctors who had experimented on prisoners were convicted, and from this came the Nuremberg Code (1947), whose first point requires the voluntary consent of anyone taking part in an experiment. But the abuses went on. In 1972 the press exposed the Tuskegee study: since 1932, the United States Public Health Service had followed the course of syphilis in hundreds of poor Black men in Alabama without telling them what they had and without curing them, not even when, around 1947, penicillin became the standard treatment. So that it would not happen again, a government commission published the <strong>Belmont Report</strong> in 1979, with three principles for research involving people: <strong>respect for persons</strong> (treating them as autonomous beings and protecting those who cannot decide for themselves), <strong>beneficence</strong> (doing no harm and seeking the greatest possible benefit) and <strong>justice</strong> (sharing burdens and benefits fairly, so that it is not always the poor who run the risks and others who enjoy the advances). That same year, Beauchamp and Childress extended these principles from research to the whole of healthcare.</p>\n<p><strong>The four principles in practice.</strong> <strong>Autonomy</strong> asks us to respect what a competent patient decides about their own body, even if the doctor thinks it a mistake: an adult may refuse an operation. <strong>Non-maleficence</strong> is the oldest rule in medicine —the <em>Epidemics</em> of the Hippocratic corpus (book I, 11) already asked doctors to help or, at least, to do no harm— and it forbids, for example, subjecting someone to useless and risky tests. <strong>Beneficence</strong> goes a step further: it obliges us to actively seek the patient’s good. And <strong>justice</strong> looks at everyone at once: how to allocate organs for transplant, intensive care beds or extremely expensive drugs when there are not enough for all. What is interesting is that the principles clash. If a patient refuses a treatment that would save them, autonomy and beneficence pull in opposite directions; in an epidemic, justice may call for individual freedoms to be limited. Beauchamp and Childress do not settle in advance which weighs more: they have to be weighed up in each case. Here we can see that the theories in this topic do not exclude one another but complement one another: autonomy recalls Kant; beneficence, utilitarian calculation; justice, Rawls. And hospital ethics committees, where health professionals, lawyers and members of the public deliberate together over a difficult case, look very much like the dialogue Habermas proposed.</p>\n<p><strong>Informed consent.</strong> This is the concrete form autonomy takes in a health centre: before an operation, a risky test or joining a clinical trial, the patient must agree after having understood. For that “yes” to count, four conditions are required. The first is enough <strong>information</strong>: what it involves, what risks it carries and what alternatives there are. The second is that the patient <strong>understands</strong> it: a form full of jargon signed in a hurry is not enough. The third is <strong>voluntariness</strong>: deciding without pressure or deception. The fourth is the <strong>capacity</strong> to decide, which may be lacking in a baby, in someone unconscious or in a person with advanced dementia; then a representative decides, and must look for what the patient would have wanted or, if that is not known, their best interest. In Spain this is regulated by Law 41/2002 on patient autonomy, which also recognises the right not to be informed and to withdraw consent at any time. Behind this lies a deep change of mentality. For centuries medical <strong>paternalism</strong> prevailed: the doctor, like a good father, knew what was good for the patient and decided for them, sometimes even hiding the diagnosis. Today the patient is understood to be an adult who decides about their own life, and the professional advises them but does not take their place.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about:</strong> Spanish law generally lets anyone aged sixteen or over decide about their own health; but if the treatment involves a serious risk to their life or health, their legal representative decides, after hearing and taking into account the minor’s opinion. Now imagine a fifteen-year-old girl with a rare heart disease. She is offered an experimental gene-editing treatment using CRISPR: it could correct the disease directly in her genes, but nobody knows its long-term effects. The other option is to carry on with the usual treatment: little risk, but the disease remains. Who should make the final decision: the doctors, the parents or the fifteen-year-old patient herself? Is a great risk acceptable if there is a chance of a cure? Some will say that maturity does not arrive all at once with a birthday, and that nobody knows better than she does what it is like to live with that disease; others, that decisions with no way back call for more protection, not less.</p>\n<p><strong>Euthanasia: an open debate.</strong> It is best to start with the distinctions, because many discussions get tangled up in words. <strong>Euthanasia</strong> (from the Greek <em>eu</em>, well, and <em>thánatos</em>, death) is when a health professional causes the death of a patient who asks for it expressly and repeatedly, to put an end to suffering they find unbearable; in <strong>assisted suicide</strong>, the professional provides the means and it is the patient who takes it. Something else again is the <strong>withholding or withdrawal of treatment</strong>, stopping or not starting treatments that only prolong a death agony, which medicine generally accepts, as it does <strong>palliative sedation</strong> to relieve pain in the last days. For the record: the Netherlands and Belgium legalised euthanasia in 2002, and Spain did so with Organic Law 3/2021, which requires being of age, suffering from a serious and incurable illness or a serious, chronic and disabling condition, asking for it in writing at least twice, and review by a commission; professionals may invoke conscientious objection. Those who defend it appeal to autonomy (nobody should be forced to live through suffering they do not want) and to compassion. Those who oppose it appeal to the dignity of every life, to the fear that elderly, disabled or lonely people may feel they are a burden and come under pressure, to the risk that the criteria will loosen over the years (the so-called slippery slope), and to the idea that the urgent task would be to guarantee good palliative care for everyone. Both positions claim to defend dignity: what does each mean by it?</p>\n<p><strong>Abortion: another open debate.</strong> The philosophical discussion turns above all on two questions. The first is the moral status of the embryo or foetus: is it already a person with a right to life from conception, only from a certain point in its development, or only after birth? The second is what happens when its life comes into conflict with the pregnant woman’s rights over her body and her life plans. Those who defend protection of the unborn stress that development is continuous, with no clear leap from one day to the next, and that the embryo is a human being on its way; Don Marquis (1989) argued that killing is wrong because it deprives someone of a valuable future like ours, and that this applies to the foetus too. Those who defend the woman’s right to choose reply that an embryo a few weeks old feels nothing and has no consciousness, and that nobody can be forced to put their body at the service of another: Judith Jarvis Thomson (1971) imagined someone who wakes up connected to a famous violinist who, to survive, needs their kidneys for nine months. Would they be obliged to stay connected? Between the two extremes there are gradualist positions, which grant the foetus more protection the further the pregnancy advances. For the record: in Spain, since 2010, a time-limit law has allowed pregnancy to be terminated at the woman’s request during the first fourteen weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Gene editing: curing or enhancing?</strong> In 2012, Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier described a technique, <strong>CRISPR</strong>, that makes it possible to cut and modify DNA with a precision and at a cost unknown until then; in 2020 they received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it. The ethical questions depend on two distinctions. The first separates <strong>somatic editing</strong>, which modifies the cells of a person already born in order to treat their illness and whose effects end with them, from <strong>germline editing</strong>, which modifies embryos and whose changes would be inherited by their children and their children’s children. The second separates <strong>therapy</strong> (curing or preventing a disease) from <strong>enhancement</strong> (making someone taller, stronger or, if it were ever possible, cleverer). In November 2018, the Chinese researcher He Jiankui announced the birth of twin girls whose embryos he had edited to make them resistant to HIV. The scientific community condemned him almost unanimously: there was no medical need, the risks were unknown and the parents had not been properly informed. In 2019 a Chinese court sentenced him to three years in prison. The underlying debate remains open. Julian Savulescu (2001) argued that, if we can choose, parents have reasons to give their children the best chances in life; Jürgen Habermas (<em>The Future of Human Nature</em>, 2001) warned, on the other hand, that a person designed by others might not feel fully the author of their own life. Where would you draw the line?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: do you have duties towards people who will be alive in a hundred years’ time and whom you will never know? If you do, how much of your comfort today would you be willing to give up for them? And if you do not, why does it seem wrong to us to leave them a worse planet?</p>\n<p><strong>Is technology neutral?</strong> People often say that a knife can be used to cut bread or to wound, and that it all depends on who uses it. But many technologies already carry decisions inside them. The historian Melvin Kranzberg summed this up in 1986 in his first law: technology is neither good nor bad, but nor is it neutral. Two cases show it. A farming company holds the patent on some genetically modified seeds that give bigger harvests, but the farmers who use them cannot replant the grain from their own harvest: they have to buy it every year. Choosing them gives more economic security now; rejecting them keeps the farmers independent. Is it legitimate for a company to hold a patent on a form of life, such as a seed? How does this affect a community’s food sovereignty, that is, its ability to decide what it produces and what it eats? In another case, a company wants to install giant machines on the coast to collect plastic from the sea, but biologists warn that they will also catch plankton and small fish. Which is better: solving a problem already created with technology, or avoiding creating it by cutting down on plastic, even if the result is slower? Evgeny Morozov (2013) called the belief that every human problem has a technical fix <strong>technological solutionism</strong>. And here Hans Jonas’s warning returns: the more power technology has, the further our responsibility reaches.</p>\n<p><strong>Who answers when a machine decides?</strong> A fourteen-year-old girl sees a humiliating video of a friend of hers on a social network, and realises that the platform’s algorithm pushes this kind of content because it gets lots of views. She can share it and get views, or report it and stand by her friend, even if she loses popularity. Who is responsible: the person who filmed it, the person who shared it or the platform itself? Are algorithms neutral, or does somebody decide how they work? These questions have a name. Dennis Thompson (1980) spoke of the <strong>problem of many hands</strong>: when an outcome depends on many people, each contributes so little that it seems nobody is to blame. Andreas Matthias (2004) added the <strong>responsibility gap</strong>: if a system learns by itself and acts in a way nobody programmed or could foresee, who is to blame? In 2018, a self-driving test car hit and killed a woman in Arizona; the software did not identify her in time, and it was the safety driver, distracted by her phone, who had to answer before the courts. Bias adds another difficulty: an algorithm learns from data from the past and can repeat its injustices. That same year it emerged that Amazon had dropped a recruitment program because it penalised CVs containing the word “women’s”, as in “women’s chess club”: it had learnt from a workforce that was almost entirely male. That is why people ask for these systems to be explainable and for a human being to oversee important decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Information ethics: what do they know about you?</strong> Every search, every “like” and every location of your phone leaves a trail of data that is worth money, because it makes it possible to predict what you will buy or what will persuade you. Hence a much-repeated saying: if a service is free, you are the product. In 2018 it emerged that the consultancy Cambridge Analytica had obtained data from up to 87 million Facebook users, most of them without knowing it, in order to target them with personalised political propaganda. That same year the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) began to apply in the European Union; it sets strict conditions on the use of personal data —often, consent— and recognises, among other things, the right to see one’s data and to ask for it to be erased; in Spain, the minimum age for giving that consent online is fourteen. The most common objection to all this is “I have nothing to hide”. The legal scholar Daniel Solove (2007) replied that privacy is not only for hiding crimes: it protects the space in which we think, make mistakes and change without being watched, and it limits the power that knowing everything about us gives to others. Anyone who knows they are being watched behaves differently. Would you accept your school reading your messages if that prevented cases of bullying? There two real goods clash, security and privacy, and neither can be entirely sacrificed to the other.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: the ethics of care reminds us that morality begins in relationships of dependence, and applied ethics translates theories into shared principles for deciding concrete cases.</strong></p>\n<h2>12. Review: four ways of judging an action</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Central question</th><th>What makes an action good</th><th>Authors</th><th>Faced with the Heinz dilemma</th><th>Usual criticism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Virtue</td><td>What would a good person do?</td><td>Springing from a virtuous character guided by prudence</td><td>Aristotle; today, Alasdair MacIntyre (<em>After Virtue</em>, 1981)</td><td>Asks what a just and brave husband would do, between resignation and violence</td><td>Gives little concrete guidance in a dilemma</td></tr><tr><td>Duty</td><td>What is my obligation?</td><td>Being done out of duty, according to a universalisable maxim</td><td>Kant</td><td>Asks whether his maxim could be a universal law; Kantians disagree about the answer</td><td>Rigidity when duties clash</td></tr><tr><td>Consequences</td><td>What produces the most well-being?</td><td>The best balance of happiness for everyone affected</td><td>Bentham, Mill, Singer</td><td>Steal, if saving a life weighs more than the harm caused</td><td>It can sacrifice individuals for the total</td></tr><tr><td>Care</td><td>What does the person who depends on me need?</td><td>Responding to the concrete needs of others</td><td>Gilligan, Noddings, Tronto</td><td>Look for ways out that do not break relationships: talk, ask for help, not leave her alone</td><td>It can favour those close over those far away</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Key idea: virtue, duty, consequences and care do not always exclude one another; many real decisions combine several viewpoints, and knowing which one you are using helps you to reason better.</strong></p>\n<p>To revise, a single case seen from the four theories. You are riding home on your scooter, you skid and scratch the car of a neighbour you hardly know. Nobody has seen you, and leaving a note with your number could cost you three hundred euros, your savings from the summer. <strong>Virtue ethics</strong> asks what an honest and courageous person would do: not hide, even if it hurts, and do it without a fuss, with the note and an apology. <strong>Duty ethics</strong> asks whether your maxim, ‘when I damage something that is not mine and nobody sees me, I will leave’, could become a universal law: if everyone followed it, nobody could count on finding their car intact; and running away would mean treating your neighbour as a mere obstacle to your money. The <strong>utilitarian</strong> does the sums: your three hundred euros against hers, which are the same amount, plus her annoyance at not knowing who did it and the small erosion of trust between neighbours; in the long run, the note comes out ahead. The <strong>ethics of care</strong> looks at the relationship: she is your neighbour, you will see her in the entrance hall, and perhaps the best thing is to knock on her door, talk and work out together a way to pay. The four agree on the answer and differ on the reasons. Nietzsche would add a suspicion: do you leave the note because it is right, or for fear of being found out, as in the ring of Gyges? Something to think about: change one detail, that the neighbour is very rich and you do not have a single euro, and see which theory changes its answer.</p>\n<p>In practice, almost nobody first chooses a theory and then applies it: we start from <strong>intuitions</strong> about cases (this is cruel, that is unjust) and look for principles that put them in order. When a principle clashes with a firm intuition, we revise one of the two. John Rawls called this back-and-forth <strong>reflective equilibrium</strong> (<em>A Theory of Justice</em>, 1971). And many ethical questions, when they become collective (how to share out, whom to protect, who decides), turn into political questions: these are the questions of <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t6\">Topic 6</a>.</p>\n<h2>13. Adela Cortina’s civic ethics</h2>\n<p>The Valencian philosopher <strong>Adela Cortina</strong> (b. 1947), professor of Ethics, asks what morality can be shared by people who think very differently about the good life. Her answer distinguishes two levels (an idea she develops in <em>Ética mínima</em>, 1986). The <strong>ethics of minimums</strong> brings together what we can <strong>demand</strong> of everyone because it is a matter of <strong>justice</strong>: basic rights, respect for dignity, equality. The <strong>ethics of maximums</strong> are the projects of a <strong>good and happy life</strong> —a religion, a tradition, an ideal— that each person or group may <strong>propose and invite others to share</strong>, but never impose.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Ethics of minimums</th><th>Ethics of maximums</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What is it about?</td><td>About justice: what is right for everyone</td><td>About happiness: the good life each person seeks</td></tr><tr><td>What does it allow?</td><td>Demanding: it binds everyone</td><td>Proposing and inviting: it cannot be imposed</td></tr><tr><td>Who shares it?</td><td>The whole of society, despite its differences</td><td>Each person or group, according to their convictions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>On those shared minimums a <strong>civic ethics</strong> is built: the common morality of a <strong>pluralist</strong> society, which does not oblige everyone to believe the same thing, but does require respect for certain shared values —freedom, equality, solidarity, active respect and dialogue— and for human rights. It is not the morality of a party or of a church: it is the ground that makes it possible to live together without giving up one’s own convictions. Cortina also works on this in relation to religious beliefs in <em>Ética civil y religión</em> (1995).</p>\n<h3>Dialogical ethics: Apel and Habermas</h3>\n<p>Cortina develops these ideas out of the <strong>discourse ethics</strong> of the German philosophers <strong>Karl-Otto Apel</strong> and <strong>Jürgen Habermas</strong>. Her thesis, in a few words: a norm is only <strong>just</strong> if <strong>all those affected</strong> by it could <strong>accept it</strong> after a <strong>dialogue</strong> in which each one argues on equal terms, with no deception or coercion. Moral truth is neither imposed from above nor decided by each person on their own: it is sought among everyone, giving and asking for reasons. Cortina adds that this dialogue is not just cold calculation, but needs mutual recognition; she calls it a <strong>cordial reason</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>What does it take for a dialogue to count?</strong> Habermas does not trust just any conversation: an agreement extracted by threats, bought, or reached because those affected never found out proves nothing. In <em>Moral Consciousness and Communicative Action</em> (1983), taking up a proposal by the legal theorist Robert Alexy, he lists the rules that anyone who argues takes for granted. Anyone capable of speaking and acting may take part. Each participant may question any assertion, put forward any proposal and express their wishes and needs. And nobody may be prevented from exercising these rights by pressure, either inside or outside the conversation. He calls this situation the <strong>ideal speech situation</strong>: in it only the force of the better argument counts. Think of a class meeting to set the date of a test. If the class rep decides beforehand with his friends, if whoever disagrees is ridiculed in the WhatsApp group, or if those who work in the afternoons cannot attend, the outcome is not a real agreement, even if it is put to a vote. No real meeting fully meets these rules, and Habermas knows it: they serve him as a yardstick for measuring how far each actual dialogue falls short of them.</p>\n<p><strong>Two principles in plain words.</strong> Discourse ethics can be summed up in two principles. <strong>Principle D</strong> (for discourse) says that a norm can only be considered valid if all those affected by it could give it their agreement as participants in a practical discourse. <strong>Principle U</strong> (for universalisation) specifies how this is tested: a norm is valid if the consequences that its general observance would have for the interests of each person could be accepted by all without coercion. Compare this with Kant. Kant asks each person to examine alone whether they could will their maxim as a universal law: it is a <strong>monological</strong> test, carried out in one’s own head. Habermas makes it <strong>dialogical</strong>: it is not enough for me to imagine what others would accept, because I may be wrong about their interests or deceive myself; they have to be asked. An example: a school wants to ban mobile phones at break time. From the standpoint of discourse ethics, it is not enough for the head teacher to be convinced it is for the best; pupils, families and teachers would have to be heard, and the rule would only be valid if all of them, informed and free from pressure, could accept its effects. Everyone need not like it, but nobody should have a weighty reason to reject it.</p>\n<p><strong>Apel: the community we all presuppose.</strong> Karl-Otto Apel (1922-2017) sought an ultimate foundation for discourse ethics in <em>Transformation of Philosophy</em> (1973). His starting point is that whoever argues, even in order to deny morality, has already entered a game with rules: they claim that their reasons hold for others, accept that they may be asked for evidence, and take for granted that others can understand and answer them. Apel calls this set of presuppositions the <strong>ideal communication community</strong>, and contrasts it with the <strong>real community</strong>, the actual one, with its inequalities, its haste and its interests. The ethical task is to bring the real closer to the ideal. To show that these presuppositions cannot be denied, Apel appeals to <strong>performative contradiction</strong>: one that lies not in what is said, but between what is said and the act of saying it. Someone who declares in the middle of a debate that “nobody has the right to contradict me” contradicts themselves, because by debating they already acknowledge that others may do so; just like someone who says “I am not speaking”. Apel also stressed <strong>co-responsibility</strong>: in an age when technology has effects on the whole planet, all of us who are able to engage in dialogue are jointly answerable for the consequences of what we decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Three ways of constructing what is just: Kant, Rawls and Habermas.</strong> These three authors are often grouped among the ethics of duty, and they have something in common: none of them derives morality from an idea of happiness. All three propose a <strong>procedure</strong>, a way of checking whether a norm is just, which is why they are called <strong>procedural</strong> ethics. Rawls called it <strong>constructivism</strong> (“Kantian Constructivism in Moral Theory”, 1980): the principles of justice are not discovered in the way a fact of nature is discovered, but constructed by means of a procedure that represents people as free and equal. What changes is who carries out the test. In <strong>Kant</strong>, each person, alone, asks whether their maxim could be a universal law. In <strong>Rawls</strong>, we imagine parties who choose the rules of society without knowing what place they will occupy in it (this is the veil of ignorance from Topic 6). In <strong>Habermas</strong>, the test is no longer imagined: those affected really carry it out, by talking. Each step tries to correct a risk in the one before. Kant’s is mistaking what seems reasonable to me for what is reasonable for everyone; Rawls’s, that the imagined parties look too much like the person imagining them. And Habermas’s, as you will see in a moment, is that real dialogues are never as clean as his rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Criticisms of discourse ethics.</strong> The first is that of <strong>idealism</strong>: in real dialogues, money, free time, the confidence to speak in public or the number of followers all carry weight, and whoever has most power tends to win even without the best argument. Habermas replies that this is exactly why the ideal situation describes no actual meeting, but serves to criticise them all. The second concerns <strong>those who cannot speak</strong>: babies, people with a severe intellectual disability, animals and future generations cannot sit down to talk, even though the norms affect them enormously. Spokespeople would have to be accepted to speak for them, but then the agreement is no longer that of all those affected. The third is that some moral disagreements may never be settled by talking, because people start from different deep convictions, and a rule that demands consensus can leave everything deadlocked. The fourth was raised by Seyla Benhabib (1987): an ethics that treats everyone as a “generalised other”, any participant with equal rights, may forget the “concrete other”, with their own history and needs; this is the objection of the ethics of care, and Cortina tries to answer it with her cordial reason. Something to think about: in your group of friends, are decisions made by the best argument or by whoever talks most? Which of Habermas’s rules is broken most often?</p>\n<h3>Aporophobia</h3>\n<p>It is usual to order human rights into three generations, a classification proposed by the jurist Karel Vasak in the late 1970s:</p><ul><li><strong>The first</strong> brings together civil and political rights: freedoms of expression, conscience and assembly, voting or a fair trial. They respond to the value of freedom and require a rule-of-law state that does not trample on it.</li><li><strong>The second</strong> brings together economic, social and cultural rights: education, health care, work and housing. It responds to equality, because someone who cannot read or has nothing to eat can hardly make use of their freedom of expression. These rights give real content to freedom.</li><li><strong>The third</strong> brings together the rights to peace, to a healthy environment and to development. It responds to solidarity and points towards a cosmopolitan order, because no country can guarantee them alone.</li></ul><p>Every right implies duties. If I have a right to education, others must not only not prevent it, but make it possible: schools, teachers and budgets are needed. That duty is fulfilled by the State through the constitution, laws and budgets. It is also fulfilled by civil society, with solidarity organisations that reach where the State does not.</p><p>Alasdair MacIntyre launched in <em>After Virtue</em> (1981) a provocative objection: human rights would be fictions, and believing in them would be like believing in witches or unicorns, because all attempts to demonstrate that they exist have failed. One can respond that they are not things found in nature, but a realistic utopia: a horizon that indicates the minimum conditions of a dignified life and allows us to criticise what fails today.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: does a right that is not fulfilled cease to exist? If in a place there are no schools, do its children not have a right to education, or do they have a right that is being denied to them?</p>\n<p>Cortina coined this word back in the 1990s (its first documented use is a column in <em>ABC Cultural</em>, 1995; from the Greek <em>áporos</em>, poor, and <em>fóbos</em>, fear) to name the <strong>rejection, fear or aversion towards the poor and the destitute</strong>, and she developed it in <em>Aporofobia, el rechazo al pobre</em> (2017), the book that made her famous. Her uncomfortable thesis: often what we call racism or xenophobia is, at bottom, aporophobia. The rich foreigner who comes to invest is not rejected, nor is the tourist, but the one who arrives without resources and seems to ‘contribute nothing’. That same year, 2017, the word was added to the dictionary of the Real Academia Española.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: a stadium cheers a foreign footballer and, at the same time, there are people who do not want a poor immigrant from the same country as a neighbour. Is it the nationality that bothers them, or the poverty?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘ethics of minimums’ does not mean an <strong>easy, weak or low-effort</strong> morality, a ‘settling for little’. The minimums are <strong>demanding</strong>: respecting everyone’s dignity, not discriminating, defending rights. ‘Minimums’ does not measure the level of demand, but the <strong>extent</strong>: it is the few things on which, being so different, we have to agree in order to be able to live together. The maximums, by contrast, are not ‘more morality’, but proposals of happiness that no one can force you to make your own.</p>\n<p>You can practise this distinction in the activity <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5-minimos-maximos\">‘An ethics of minimums or of maximums?’</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: for Cortina a plural society does not need everyone to share the same idea of happiness (maximums), but some minimums of justice that can be demanded of all; civic ethics is built on them, and its most urgent opposite today is aporophobia.</strong></p>"}});
